\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan edges up vs dlr, unfazed by new U.S. pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 13:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * U.S. Democrats prod Obama to press China on yuan policy\par \par \uc2 * Argentina becomes sixth nation to do \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps with China\par \par \uc2 * Dealers see yuan remaining in narrow range\par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, March 31 (Reuters) - The yuan edged up against the dollar on Tuesday but held tightly to its recent well-worn range, unfazed by news that U.S. congressional Democrats urged President Barack Obama to press China on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy they believe keeps the yuan undervalued.\par \par \uc2 Before trading began, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed a weaker mid-point at 6.8359 against the dollar, versus Monday's reference rate of 6.8340.\par \par \uc2 Traders attributed the lower midpoint to an overnight rise in the U.S. Dollar Index <.DXY>, although the index eased during the Asian trading day on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "The market believes that global political and economic realities will prove overwhelming and that the Obama administration will have to stick to the Bush team's 'no-manipulation' label for China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said a U.S. bank dealer in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "As for the Chinese side, it undoubtedly has no intention of letting the yuan move out of its current narrow range anytime soon, given the lingering uncertainty in the global financial crisis."\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan <CNY=CFXS> ended at 6.8336 to the dollar on Tuesday, up from Monday's close of 6.8366. The rate has stayed well within a 6.82 to 6.86 range throughout the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 Offshore one-year non-deliverable dollar/yuan forwards (NDFs) <CNY1YNDFOR=> were trading at 6.7863 bid, compared with Monday's close of 6.8070.\par \par \uc2 Their latest level implied yuan appreciation of 0.73 percent against the dollar over the next 12 months from the day's spot mid-point, up from 0.40 percent appreciation implied on Monday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. COMPLAINTS\par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/yuan volatilities <CNY1YO=> were bid at 7.55 percent late on Tuesday after rising to a two-week high of 8.0 percent during the day, reflecting a sense in the market of a risk of instability in the yuan in the long term.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to Obama on Monday, top U.S. Democrats on the House of Representatives Ways and Means Committee urged the president to consider several options to increase multilateral pressure on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, including a possible case at the World Trade Organisation.\par \par \uc2 "We urge the new administration to consider a new approach," the lawmakers said in the letter, delivered one day before the U.S. Trade Representative's office was scheduled to release its annual report on foreign trade barriers.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive, prolonged, one-way and sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, its unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  accumulation ($1.9 trillion), and its massive current account surplus demonstrate that, once again, China is manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," they said. [ID:nN30364158]\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. lawmakers have complained for years that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued and gives Chinese companies an unfair price advantage. China has denied the charge, insisting that it must reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pricing mechanisms first before it makes the yuan fully convertible under the capital accounts.\par \par \uc2 China has recently joined a drive to free up trade-finance channels that have been clogged by the global credit crunch.\par \par \uc2 It has agreed to a 70 billion yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap with Argentina, the sixth country to sign such a deal with China since December. [ID:nPEK111298] (Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|Z|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|RNP|DNP|PTD|PCM|PEN|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090403e53v0010y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
