\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Has China sold dollars to support the yuan?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Simon Rabinovitch and Lu Jianxin   \par \uc2 1,084 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 09:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, March 18 (Reuters) - Long accused by critics in the West of keeping its exchange rate undervalued, China may now be tip-toeing onto the other side of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade to sell dollars and ward off depreciation pressure on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts say that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been small, barely enough to make a ripple in the market, but that it will show through with a drop in the country's stockpile of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 And few think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to support the yuan <CNY=CFXS> for long, because China's trade surplus is likely to rebound from the depressed level in February that may have prompted the move.\par \par \uc2 But the possibility that China has sold some \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to keep the yuan steady flies in the face of charges that it has consistently suppressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2 It also all but rules out any concern that Beijing might engineer a significant depreciation to support struggling exporters at the same time as its neighbours' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it points to a government that is true to its word about wanting a stable yuan, at least against the dollar, whether the day-to-day pressure is for appreciation or depreciation.\par \par \uc2 "If this were a market-determined exchange rate, it would now be weakening, because the overall balance of payments looks to be in deficit, but it is not weakening," said Paul Gruenwald, ANZ's Singapore-based chief economist for Asia.\par \par \uc2 "The implication is that authorities must be selling their dollar reserves in order to stabilise the USD-CNY exchange rate," Gruenwald, a former International Monetary Fund official, said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts estimate capital outflows from China were largely balanced in the fourth quarter and in January by inflows from its yawning trade surplus, negating pressure on the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 But a surprise slump in exports in February dragged the surplus down to $4.8 billion and, if capital outflows were more or less constant, easily tipped China's balance of payments into a deficit on the month.\par \par \uc2 Despite the current account fluctuations, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the yuan on a remarkably tight leash, even by its standards, in February, locking it at about 6.835 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 An analyst in Beijing, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because he is not authorised to speak to the media, said he had seen downward pressure in intraday trading that had required the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to wade in.\par \par \uc2 "I think they are selling every once in a while, but not necessarily consistently," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have sold $1 billion or so on several occasions to maintain the yuan's value in its narrow range -- small enough to go virtually undetected, he added.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets expect the stability to endure, with non-deliverable forwards <CNY1YNDFOR=> implying yuan depreciation of just 0.65 percent against the dollar over the next year.\par \par \uc2 (To see a graphic the yuan and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, click on: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/CN_YNS0309.gif"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://customers.reuters.com/d/graphics/CN_YNS0309.gif}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 SEARCHING FOR PROOF\par \par \uc2 China caused a stir in December by letting the yuan fall to the bottom of its trading band against the dollar on four straight days, but then quickly guided it back up, alarmed in part by the potential for capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 Many saw the brief yuan drop as a shot across U.S. bows. Beijing was reminding Washington that it was doing it a favour by keeping the yuan stable at a time when the dollar was soaring against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One way to demonstrate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to examine changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. But extensive assumptions are first needed about the composition of reserves -- a state secret -- as well as how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts for asset price and exchange rate fluctuations.\par \par \uc2 After controlling for those changes, any net drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, alongside broad stability in the exchange rate, would point to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  having sold dollars and bought yuan.\par \par \uc2 The trouble is that valuation swings can be massive. Paper losses on non-dollar assets and U.S. bond prices, for example, probably explained January's record monthly drop in reserves, disclosed by Reuters. [ID:nPEK203137]\par \par \uc2 Another big fall in February, when valuations should have steadied, would furnish clearer evidence of capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the PBOC has plenty of ways to cloak reserve shifts, so drawing definitive conclusions is fraught with uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Another key indicator of aggregate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows, under both the current and financial accounts, is monthly data on new yuan positions for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by the PBOC and commercial banks.\par \par \uc2 In January, the last month for which data is available, the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought totalled 141.4 billion yuan ($20.7 billion), down sharply from 654.1 billion yuan in January 2008. If net new yuan positions turned negative in February, it would be a strong sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had propped up the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "The figures show a clear trend that money flowing into China began to drop sharply from the fourth quarter," said Liu Junyu, a debt market analyst at \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shenzhen.\par \par \uc2 NO RUN ON THE YUAN\par \par \uc2 With the economy still geared toward exports, analysts expect China's trade surplus has probably rebounded from the abrupt narrowing in February.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, since the current account surplus, not speculative cash, has been the main driver of China's foreign reserves, few think that a wall of "hot money" is now set to leave the country.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the smaller capital inflows, China is in no danger of seeing a massive capital outflow," said Wang Haoyu, an economist at \uc2 First Capital Securities\uc2  in Shenzhen. "The yuan will still see appreciation pressure in the long term."\par \par \uc2 And as the World Bank noted in its quarterly update on the Chinese economy released on Wednesday, Beijing has a virtual fortress in its $2 trillion of foreign reserves to defend the yuan under extreme -- and extremely improbable -- circumstances.\par \par \uc2 * For more news on the yuan, click on [CN-FRX-RTRS-LEN]\par \par \uc2 * To read the daily yuan report, see [CNY/]\par \par \uc2 * For live data on yuan trade, type CNY= then F3 ($1=6.834 Yuan) (Editing by Kim Coghill; Graphic by Catherine 