\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Ends Up On Suspected PBOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2008\par 10:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.8346               6.8353 \par USD/CNY OTC 0930 GMT     6.8230    -0.17%     6.8353 \par              High        6.8347     0.00% \par              Low         6.8202    -0.21% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--Unexpectedly strong dollar sales from large Chinese banks pushed the yuan up against the U.S. unit Wednesday, the last trading day of the year, in what some dealers suspected was a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -directed effort to boost the yuan's headline appreciation figure for 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dealers said they were uncertain how the yuan would trade in the New Year, but thought the dollar-yuan central parity rate would most likely maintain its recent orbit around 6.8350.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed Thursday and Friday for the New Year holiday, and will reopen Monday.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.8230 around 0930 GMT, down from Tuesday's close of CNY6.8353. It traded between CNY6.8202 and CNY6.8347.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-yuan central parity rate was set at 6.8346, down slightly from 6.8353 Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Dollar demand has been fairly strong in recent weeks driven by demand from importers, and precautionary buying by banks uncertain whether the People's \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  might still weaken the yuan to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 Dealers also say that dollar sales have dropped off recently, in part because of China's declining exports, but also because exporters, wary the yuan might depreciate, are choosing not to convert their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings into yuan.\par \par \uc2 Against that backdrop, dealers said they were surprised by the emergence of strong demand for yuan Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "Large banks (first) started selling dollars heavily late yesterday," said a Guangzhou-based dealer with a foreign bank. "Whether that was for customers or for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hard to say."\par \par \uc2 Dealers were broadly uncertain about what drove the yuan's gains.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to say for sure, but some banks may be pushing the yuan up at the end of the year" for non-market-driven reasons, said a Shanghai-based dealer with foreign bank.\par \par \uc2 Sharing a theory also expressed by other dealers, he said the PBOC may have directed state-owned banks to push the yuan up to improve the yuan's full-year appreciation against the dollar for 2008.\par \par \uc2 Although the yuan is still about 200 points below the record level it posted against the dollar in July, it nonetheless was 7.1% higher against the dollar as of 0930 GMT compared with the start of the year. That is more than the 6.9% rise it posted for 2007.\par \par \uc2 It is also significantly higher than the 6.5% year-to-date rise it registered after falling to CNY6.8565 against the dollar Tuesday, after which the yuan started rising.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the turnaround came after someone posted an offer to sell dollars at CNY6.8500 on the automatic price-matching system mid-morning, well below the market level.\par \par \uc2 The price-matching system is a little-used anonymous trading platform that dealers believe the PBOC uses on occasion to signal its intentions to the market, while not officially showing its hand.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forward were at 7.0653/7.0753, up from 6.9900/7.0100 late Tuesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 -By Denis McMahon and Wynne Wang, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon@dowjones.com [ 31-12-08 0958GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081231e4cv00068\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
