\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan nearly flat despite big ease, outlook weaker\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin   \par \uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2008\par 11:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan almost flat against the dollar on Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced its biggest monetary easing in a decade. But traders said the easing could exert long-term downward pressure on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 About 45 minutes before the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closed, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut banks' benchmark one-year lending and deposit rates by 1.08 percentage points. It also cut banks' reserve ratios by between one and two percentage points, effective on Dec. 5. [ID:nBJC000226]\par \par \uc2 The easing came sooner than many traders had expected and was much larger than the market had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan <CNY=CFXS> barely moved after the announcement in calm trade and closed at 6.8287 against the dollar, down only marginally from Tuesday's finish of 6.8260.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use its daily reference rate system and indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to keep the yuan steady in coming days, in the narrow range of roughly 6.82-6.85 that it has maintained for the past several months.\par \par \uc2 "No one wants to fight the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "Offshore, non-deliverable forwards have already overestimated the potential for the yuan to depreciate, so the potential for NDFs to imply further yuan depreciation is small unless the dollar surges in global markets again."\par \par \uc2 Before trading started on Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan's mid-point <CNY=SAEC> against the dollar at 6.8272, barely changed from 6.8284 on Tuesday and 6.8304 on Monday. Traders took this as a signal of Chinese authorities' determination to maintain stability.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has publicly said it wants to avoid the large capital outflows suffered by some other Asian countries in recent months. This is believed to be a major motive for keeping the yuan steady.\par \par \uc2 LONG TERM\par \par \uc2 However, several dealers and analysts said such a large monetary easing could prove negative for the yuan in the long run -- both by reducing the yuan's interest rate premium over foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and by suggesting that the Chinese economy was weak.\par \par \uc2 "All my colleagues were shocked by such a big easing. It signals the government may believe the economic situation is really serious for it to call for such a drastic move," said Liu Dongliang, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shenzhen:\par \par \uc2 "No doubt it will be negative for the yuan's exchange rate in the medium and long run, though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may work to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable in coming days."\par \par \uc2 So far, China's overall exports have held up well during the slump of the global economy; annual export growth was 19.2 percent in October, and the trade surplus actually expanded as import growth slowed more dramatically.\par \par \uc2 But many smaller Chinese exporters are believed to be struggling, which could conceivably prompt a change to China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy if unemployment worsens next year.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan non-deliverable forwards <CNY1YNDFOR=> were down slightly in quiet trade to 6.9850 late on Wednesday from Tuesday's close of 6.9920.\par \par \uc2 Their latest level implied yuan depreciation of 2.26 percent from the day's spot mid-point over the next 12 months, against 2.34 percent depreciation implied at Tuesday's close.\par \par \uc2 Implied one-year depreciation rose above 5.0 percent in late October, but then pulled back as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled its determination to maintain a stable exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/yuan volatilities <CNY1YO=> continued edging down on Wednesday along with implied yuan depreciation. They fell to 11.75 percent bid from 12.00 percent at Tuesday's close, after last month hitting a multi-year high of 15.50 percent. (Editing by Andrew Torchia)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|Z|E|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|RNP|DNP|PTD|PCM|PEN|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081129e4bq00079\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
