\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  cuts Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  reserves, except  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2008\par 11:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 14 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in most Asian countries fell in September as central banks tried to support their ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid global financial turmoil, but China's cashpile continued to rise, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2  Reserves held by Asian countries excluding China fell by $20.33 billion in September to $2.46 trillion, led by declines of $12.9 billion in Malaysia, $3.53 billion in South Korea and $3.49 billion in India, according to the figures.\par \par \uc2  "It's not surprising with central banks engaging in smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid the volatile market conditions," said Han Sia Yeo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Central banks in South Korea, India, the Philippines, Thailand and Malaysia have sold dollars in recent months to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in part to limit inflationary risks.\par \par \uc2  The sharp drop in Malaysia's reserves may have been caused by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also by the valuation factor -- the dollar's relative strength against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the reserves, analysts say.\par \par \uc2  By contrast, China's reserves, the world's largest, grew by $21.45 billion in September to a record $1.91 trillion, underpinned by a record trade surplus, and foreign investment.\par \par \uc2  For all of Asia, reserves totalled $4.36 trillion at the end of September, virtually unchanged from a month earlier.\par \par \uc2  The data highlighted that while most Asian central banks are selling dollars to limit falls in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Chinese authorities are still buying dollars to slow the rise in the yuan <CNY=CFXS>, which has been kept steady since mid-July.\par \par \uc2  The rise in China's reserves - $39 billion in August and $36 billion in July -- are likely to slow further as Chinese exports take a hit from weaker global demand while the yuan's performance discourages hot money inflows, analysts say.\par \par \uc2  "It's becoming increasingly clear that the Chinese don't seem to tolerate further yuan appreciation given the slowdown of the economy," said Dwyfor Evans, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 State Street Global Markets\uc2  in Hong Kong. Latest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 \par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve holdings: Country       Reserves     As at    \par end-2007     percent\par           $ billion              $ billion      change\par  China         1905.60    Sep 30      1528.20       24.70\par  Japan          995.89    Sep 30       973.37        2.31\par  India          291.82    Sep 26       270.31        7.96\par  Taiwan         281.13    Sep 30       262.22        7.21\par  South Korea    239.67    Sep 30       275.56      -13.02\par  Singapore      168.80    Sep 30       162.95        3.59\par  Hong Kong      160.60    Sep 30       152.70        5.17\par  Malaysia       109.70    Sep 30       101.30        8.29\par  Thailand       103.80    Sep 26        87.60       18.49\par  Indonesia       57.11    Sep 29        57.04        0.12\par  Philippines     36.69    Sep 30        33.75        8.71\par  Pakistan        08.13    Sep 27        15.74      -48.35\par  Bangladesh       5.86    Sep 30         5.51        6.35\par  TOTAL        4,364.80               3,926.25       11.17\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * In April 2005, China injected $15 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves into the Industrial and Commercial Bank of\par China  , the country's largest lender, to\par help it restructure to sell stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * In December 2003, China used $45 billion of reserves to\par bail out \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2   and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par [CCB.UL] .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Those funds are not included in this figure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Sources: central banks.\par  (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ASIA-RESERVES|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|J|SNI|SNS|PHD|SI|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081014e4ae000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
