\par\par \b \uc2 China c.bank says to slow yuan rise - economist\b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2008\par 07:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TIANJIN, China, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The People's \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  has stated recently that it will slow appreciation of the yuan, a prominent economist who met with officials of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 The comments by David Hale, chairman of Hale Advisors, support the perception of many observers that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shifted to a policy of slower yuan strengthening, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stalled against the dollar in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 "I had lunch with the People's \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  on Friday, and they told me that they're going to slow down the appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  here," Hale told a meeting of the World Economic Forum in the northern Chinese port city of Tianjin.\par \par \uc2 "If they do that, they have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be providing for America next year another $200-300 billion of demand for U.S. Treasury bills," he said.\par \par \uc2 In a discussion on the future role of the dollar in the global economy, Hale cited China's policy as one reason to believe that the United States would continue to be able to cover its budget deficit, even if it carries out the massive financial bailout plan under discussion in the U.S. Congress.\par \par \uc2 The yuan closed at 6.8485 against the dollar on Friday, the last trading day of the third quarter before a week-long national holiday, little changed from the level at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 That came after the yuan appreciated about 6.5 percent in the first six months of the year. Many economists have now changed their forecasts for the yuan to account for a slower pace in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Hale added that he thought China's increasing role in the global economy would mean that the yuan, or renminbi (RMB), would one day become a global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though that would take time on account of China's capital restrictions.\par \par \uc2 "Given China's evolving role, there's no doubt the RMB will assume a global role in time," he said. "We're talking 20 years from now, not in the next few years," he said. (Reporting by Jason Subler; Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-YUAN/HALE|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080928e49s0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
