\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-China steps to boost stocks sharply, support yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin   \par \uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2008\par 16:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government may boost \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market as much as 20 percent in coming weeks while supporting the yuan's exchange rate and slowing a steep fall in market interest rates.\par \par \uc2 In the past several weeks, Chinese financial markets have been caught in a triple play of plunging equities, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness and sliding bond yields.\par \par \uc2 By using government money to buy shares from the market, authorities stand a good chance of breaking this cycle and reducing the risk of capital flight from China, fund managers, analysts and traders said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The decision to buy shares in major banks is a signal of support for the market, and it shows confidence the shares are decently valued," said Wu Haijun, Shanghai principal at Power Pacific Corp of Canada,a foreign investor in Chinese stocks.\par \par \uc2 "The market-boosting steps should allow the stock index to rebound around 20 percent over the next several weeks."\par \par \uc2 The government announced after the markets closed on Thursday that Central Huijin, an arm of the country's sovereign wealth fund, would help stabilise the stock market by buying shares in listed companies, including three top state-owned banks.\par \par \uc2 It is the first time in the nearly 18-year history of China's modern stock market that authorities have announced a central government agency will buy stocks in order to support the market.\par \par \uc2 Authorities also scrapped the 0.1 percent stamp tax on purchases of equities, and urged state-owned enterprises to buy back shares of their listed units from the market. (for story, click on)\par \par \uc2 PLUNGE\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai index has plunged 69 percent from last October's record peak and hit a 22-month closing low on Thursday. About $3 trillion of capitalisation on the Shanghai and Shenzhen stock exchanges has been wiped out since December.\par \par \uc2 The government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not remove key reasons for the slide: a huge overhang of stock becoming newly tradable as lock-up periods for controlling and institutional shareholders expire, and slowing growth in the economy and corporate profits.\par \par \uc2 But by waiting until the market had dropped to levels where stocks were no longer expensive by historical standards, authorities have maximised the chances of their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeding, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The average price-earnings ratio of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  A shares is at a record low of 16 times historic earnings, last hit in late 2005 when the market was ending a four-year slump.\par \par \uc2 "The market will jump at least 5 percent tomorrow, and it is likely to continue the rebound for at least several weeks since investors will expect further market-friendly policies if needed," said Qian Qimin, analyst at Shenyin & Wanguo Securities.\par \par \uc2 The government did not say how many shares it would buy, but Huijin has access to about two-thirds of the assets of \uc2 China Investment Corp\uc2 , which was set up a year ago to manage $200 billion of China's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe that if Huijin's buying does not succeed in putting a floor under the market, the government could announce fresh restrictions on the sale of shares freed up by the expiry of lock-up periods.\par \par \uc2 YUAN\par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the yuan largely stable against the dollar in the spot market, the weakness of China's stock and property markets helped push the yuan down sharply in the offshore forwards market this month.\par \par \uc2 This week, after a surprise monetary easing by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , non-deliverable forwards began for the first time in five years to imply yuan depreciation against the dollar over the next 12 months. This threatened to encourage capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 "The stock market support steps should give the yuan a shot in the arm, as they will dampen expectations for major capital outflows," said a senior dealer at a North American bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 China's five-year government bond yield has tumbled 70 basis points over the past five weeks as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eased monetary policy and as money has fled the stock market in favour of bonds.\par \par \uc2 Duan Yunfei, bond analyst at \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shenzhen, said the stock market rescue would probably not halt the downtrend in yields over the medium term, because China had apparently entered a monetary easing cycle.\par \par \uc2 But a stock market rebound in coming weeks could slow the bond rally by drawing money back to equities, he said. And if stocks do strengthen, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may feel less pressure to ease policy quickly. (Editing by Andrew Torchia and David Cowell)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -MARKETS/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080921e49i000ci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
