\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Calls mount for China to temper yuan appreciation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason Subler   \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2008\par 07:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in paragraphs 17-18)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, April 28 (Reuters) - China should keep the yuan fairly stable to anchor expectations about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate of climb, a prominent economist said in remarks published on Monday, adding to a growing tide of calls for it to rise more slowly.\par \par \uc2 But a separate government think-tank suggested that Beijing should let the yuan float freely, underscoring divisions in policy circles since the yuan has stabilised around 7 per dollar following several months of quickened appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Xia Bin, a researcher with the Development Research Centre, was quoted by the official China Securities Journal as saying a stronger yuan could be of only limited use in the near term in fighting inflation, which is near 12-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the absence of a developed set of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, firms were having to combat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk by shortening order times, which was not conducive to the development of the export sector, Xia said.\par \par \uc2 His institute reports to the State Council, China's cabinet.\par \par \uc2 "We should take adjustment to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy as a key task, stabilise market expectations towards the exchange rate and give the market a relatively stable signal on the exchange rate," he was quoted as saying.\par \par \uc2 Xia's note of caution echoed comments by Zhang Xiaoqiang, vice-chairman of the National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC), the economic planning agency.\par \par \uc2 Zhang said that the rising yuan was a concern for many businesses, and recommended that the government take steps to stabilise expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Some exporters don't even want to take export orders for the third quarter because they have no idea how much the yuan will appreciate," he told a conference over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 TOUGH TIMES\par \par \uc2 The People's \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying most of the dollars flowing into China in order to control the yuan's ascent.\par \par \uc2 To help tackle inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to tamp down prices by permitting the yuan, also known as the renminbi (RMB), to climb at an annualised rate of about 17 percent in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 But with clouds gathering over the export sector, the rise has virtually ground to a halt in April. The yuan marked time on Monday around 7 per dollar, marking a total gain of 18 percent since China abandoned a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar in July 2005.\par \par \uc2 Fan Jianping, an economist at the State Information Centre, a think-tank under the NDRC, also said last week that Beijing should break expectations of a speedy yuan rise and stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid causing mass unemployment.\par \par \uc2 However, He Fan with the Research Center for International Finance, part of the top government think-tank, the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, offered a radically different view.\par \par \uc2 He said in a report on Monday that China should cut the yuan loose, because otherwise there would always be an incentive for speculators to bet on a further rise.\par \par \uc2 "We think that under the current circumstances, temporarily allowing the yuan's exchange rate to float completely freely can successfully stamp out the market's one-sided expectations about the yuan's appreciation," He said.\par \par \uc2 Frank Gong, chief China economist at \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 , said Beijing was weighing the pros and cons of a one-off revaluation of 10-15 percent in coming months. That would probably explain why the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has temporarily paused around 7 per dollar in the last week or so, he said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Gong said the chances of such a move were still less than 50 percent. But he added: "At the very least, we expect the RMB to continue its accelerated pace of appreciation to reach 6.3 against the dollar by the end of this year." (Reporting by Jason Subler; Editing by Alan Wheatley)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080428e44s000hx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
