\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan ends up as c.bank signals no policy change   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin   \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2008\par 10:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Feb 14 (Reuters) - The yuan closed up against the dollar on Thursday after the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a higher mid-point, which was seen as a signal that China's policy of faster yuan appreciation this year would not change despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unexpected plunge on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> finished at 7.1904 on Thursday, up from 7.2000 at the close on Wednesday, when the yuan fell sharply from its previous finish of 7.1840. At one stage on Wednesday, the yuan was down 0.60 percent to a low of 7.2270, its biggest daily drop since it was revalued in July 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Wednesday's tumble briefly sparked rumours in overseas markets about Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  might want to slow yuan appreciation as the U.S. economy weakened.   \par \par \uc2 But onshore traders rejected such rumours, saying the yuan's plunge was merely due to exceptionally thin trading conditions and the temporary absence of exporters, many of which had not yet returned from long Lunar New Year holidays.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to confirm on Thursday that the yuan's uptrend had not changed by fixing its daily mid-point <CNY=SAEC> at 7.1890, up from Wednesday's 7.1952 and near the mid-point's post-revaluation peak of 7.1846, hit on Feb. 5.   \par \par \uc2 "The higher mid-point today after yesterday's volatility shows the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains committed to faster appreciation of the yuan in the medium term," said a dealer at a major Chinese state-owned bank in Beijing.   \par \par \uc2 Several dealers said they were maintaining their view that the yuan could breach the psychologically important 7.0000 level as soon as in roughly two weeks, and appreciate more than 8.5 percent this year compared with a rise of 6.86 percent in 2007.   \par \par \uc2 Wednesday's plunge briefly took the yuan close to the lower edge of its daily trading band, an extremely rare event. The band is 0.5 percent on either side of the day's mid-point.   \par \par \uc2 "In the past, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan and keep it stable," said a dealer at a Chinese commercial bank in Shenzhen.   \par \par \uc2 "It didn't do so yesterday, perhaps because it's now happy to see more volatility. But that doesn't mean the yuan's longer-term outlook has changed."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to want to increase daily volatility to stimulate the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and accustom Chinese institutions to managing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.   \par \par \uc2 In the offshore market, one-year dollar/yuan non-deliverable forwards <CNY1YNDFOR=> were little changed at 6.6060/6.6110 late on Thursday, compared with 6.6060/6.6130 on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Their latest levels implied yuan appreciation of 8.74-8.83 percent from Thursday's mid-point over the next 12 months, against 8.80-8.92 percent implied on Wednesday. (Editing by Anne Marie Roantree   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-EARLY|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080217e42e0008k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
