\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan hits new high vs dlr after inflation warning   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin   \par \uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2008\par 10:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The yuan set a fresh post-revaluation high against the dollar on Wednesday after a warning about inflation by Premier Wen Jiabao reinforced the view that China would have to appreciate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rapidly.   \par \par \uc2 Before trade began, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its daily mid-point <CNY=SAEC> at 7.2723, up from Tuesday's reference rate of 7.2791 but lower than Tuesday's traded close of 7.2643.   \par \par \uc2 The mid-point implied "that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to see the yuan rise steadily, but not too fast", said a dealer at a European bank in Shanghai.   \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> rose as far as 7.2632 in the opening minutes and traded slightly below that level for most of the day. But it shot up to a new post-revaluation high of 7.2614 in late trade after Wen's remarks, before closing at 7.2620.   \par \par \uc2 Wen told a cabinet meeting that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  still faced fairly large inflationary pressures and would temporarily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in goods markets to stabilise prices for basic necessities.   \par \par \uc2 As with previous such official statements, the pledge to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep down prices was taken by financial markets more as a signal that the inflation threat was serious, than as reassurance that authorities would soon succeed in taming inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Several dealers predicted the yuan would hit 7.25 as soon as this week as China's need to fight inflation, as well as international pressure from its trading partners, encouraged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to engineer appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/yuan non-deliverable forwards <CNY1YNDFOR=> were marginally higher at 6.6830/90 in late trade, implying 12-month appreciation from Wednesday's mid-point of 8.72-8.82 percent.   \par \par \uc2 One-year dollar/yuan volatilities <CNY1YO=> remained at Tuesday's seven-week closing low of 3.90 percent bid. One-year vols have tumbled from around 5.0 percent this week, suggesting the market has reached a consensus on the pace of appreciation after a period of uncertainty caused by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's acceleration of appreciation in the past two months. (Editing by Andrew Torchia)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|Z|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080112e4190006j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
