\par\par \b \uc2 China Trade Surplus Decrease May Endanger GDP Growth - Report   \b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2008\par 05:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--The expected slowdown in the growth of China's trade surplus in 2008 must be controlled to safeguard the country's economic growth, the official China Securities Journal reported Thursday, citing former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser Yu Yongding.   \par \par \uc2 China's GDP growth will fall by three percentage points this year from the expected 11.4% in 2007 if the trade surplus remains at the 2007 level of US$300 billion, said Yu, a well-known economist and a former member of the People's  \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  Monetary Policy Committee.   \par \par \uc2 This would mean GDP growth falling below 9%, which Yu describes as the "necessary lowest limit for China's economic growth," according to the report. Yu didn't elaborate.   \par \par \uc2 China's GDP grew 11.5% in the first three quarters of 2007 over the same period a year earlier, and is expected to grow 11.4% for the full year, according to government forecast. It is widely believed that China's export and trade surplus growth will decrease in 2008 due to U.S.-led global economic slowdown. But China still expects a GDP increase of at least 9% according to government think tank the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences.   \par \par \uc2 Yu said China should stimulate more domestic demand to offset the negative impact of falling external demand.   \par \par \uc2 Yu also urged the People's  \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to increase the flexibility of the yuan exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Newspaper Web 