\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: China To Step Up Loan Curbs To Cool Econ - PBOC Memo   \b0\par\par\uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2007\par 11:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more details of planned credit controls.)   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China plans to clamp down further on runaway bank lending in 2008 to avoid harsher austerity measures that could lead to a hard landing for the world's fourth-largest economy, according to a memorandum of a recent closed-door meeting between Chinese regulators and banks.   \par \par \uc2 Under the new guidelines, banks will need to heed quarterly limits on lending, and those that exceed the limits will be penalized, according to the memo seen Tuesday by Dow Jones Newswires.   \par \par \uc2 The credit controls are the latest effort by China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the booming economy from overheating and inflation getting out of control. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reined in monetary policy this year but the economy remains stuck in high gear, partly due to super easy credit conditions. The command-and-control steps highlight the difficulty China is having in effectively using price-based tools such as interest rates to correct economic imbalances.   \par \par \uc2 "Next year is a special year with the change in government and the Beijing Olympic Games. If loans get out of control, that could lead to harsh macro controls and the biggest losers would be commercial banks," the memo said.   \par \par \uc2 The meeting, held last Thursday, was presided over by People's  \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  Assistant Gov. Yi Gang, with officials from China Banking Regulatory Commission and 21 of China's policy banks and major commercial banks in attendance.   \par \par \uc2 Under the planned loan controls, the PBOC advises banks to allocate 30%-35% of their full-year loan allotments in the first quarter, 60%-65% in the first half, 80%-85% by the end of the third quarter, with the remainder for the final quarter of the year.   \par \par \uc2 Yi told the meeting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be able to allow for loan allotments in excess of three percentage points of the maximum range for each quarter, the memo said.   \par \par \uc2 But banks that exceed the specified lending limits will pay a price. Primary market dealers will be forced to purchase special \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills equivalent to the amount exceeding their loan caps, while non-primary market dealers will have to set aside special deposits with the PBOC, the memo said.   \par \par \uc2 The special \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills will carry a maturity of one year and an interest rate of 1.89%, or the rate that the PBOC pays on required reserves. The bills can't be transferred or returned at an earlier date, the memo said.   \par \par \uc2 Despite five rate hikes and nine increases in banks' reserve requirement ratio this year by the PBOC, China's new yuan loan growth for the first 10 months of 2007 stood at CNY3.5 trillion, already exceeding the CNY3.18 trillion recorded for the entire 2006. China's total loan growth, including yuan-denominated lending, has been accelerating since June.   \par \par \uc2 Unabated growth in China's trade surplus plus a reluctance by Beijing to let the yuan rise faster to help absorb capital inflows have hurt the effectiveness of monetary policy and spurred inflation to 11-year highs.   \par \par \uc2 The country's economy grew 11.5% in the first three quarters of this year, and in 2007 is expected to outpace last year's 11.1% growth and record a fifth year of double-digit gains.   \par \par \uc2 Beijing is targeting lower economic and lending growth in 2008 than this year and banks have been asked to submit their loan plans for 2008 by Dec. 5 to the PBOC's monetary policy department, the memo said.   \par \par \uc2 A key government economic work committee has already adjusted China's monetary policy to reflect a tighter bias for next year, according to the memo.   \par \par \uc2 This year, the government has used the term "stability" to characterize how it wants to reach a moderate tightening in monetary policy. But the work committee dropped the word "stability" to signal Beijing's urgency for an even tighter bias for next year, according to the memo.   \par \par \uc2 The memo noted that China is working toward a more market-oriented economy and that the PBOC or the State Council, China's highest executive body, do not wish to issue planning or administrative objectives in these matters.   \par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shouldn't have to manage interest rates or the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, under \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current conditions it must \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in these areas, it said.   \par \par \uc2 The memo said 2008 and 2009 are critical years in China's economic cycle, but after 2009 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not need to resort to such administrative controls if the trade surplus is controlled and China's exchange rate flexibility has strengthened.   \par \par \uc2 -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; jr.wu@dowjones.com [ 27-11-07 1058GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071127e3br000k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
