\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Global powers use words to check dollar's fall   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven C. Johnson and Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 1,112 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2007\par 22:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK/LONDON, Nov 15 (Reuters) - They say actions speak louder than words, but pro-market U.S. authorities who would rather not lift a finger to prop up a declining dollar are hoping that this time, the opposite proves true.   \par \par \uc2 If it doesn't, and the dollar continues to fall, they may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since 2000, in a clash with their free market principles.   \par \par \uc2 By defending the dollar as a global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson tiptoed beyond the usual rhetoric about a strong dollar being in the U.S. interest.   \par \par \uc2 A Chinese official had earlier spooked markets by saying the dollar was losing its position as premier reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending it to the third record low versus the euro this month.   \par \par \uc2 Paulson's comment also dovetailed with complaints from Europe, Canada and Japan about abrupt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and may suggest an official willingness to use verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the dollar's steady slide from becoming a rout.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't know if it was coordinated across countries but there was an explicit decision within each country to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Stephen Jen in London.   \par \par \uc2 Global tensions over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to dominate this weekend's meeting of the Group of 20 nations in South Africa, and while few expect it to yield coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials may well agree on the need for more jawboning.   \par \par \uc2 In official circles, it appears "the green light has given way to amber," said Neil MacKinnon, chief economist at ECU Group, a London hedge fund with $1.3 billion under management.   \par \par \uc2 "If there's no verbal concern expressed by policymakers about the dollar's slide, I'm sure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will test policymakers' pain threshold and you'd see further dollar slippage," MacKinnon said.   \par \par \uc2 FREE MARKETEERS   \par \par \uc2 U.S. officials are perhaps the most hostile among Group of Seven policymakers to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, preferring to see exchange rates set in free, open and competitive markets.   \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration has been one of the most hands-off in history and proved to be the first American presidency since the gold standard was dropped in 1971 not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 That's largely because a weaker dollar is trimming the U.S. trade deficit by making exports more competitive, a helpful crutch should a persistent housing slump and tighter credit conditions continue to slow economic growth.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also make it tougher for Washington to lecture \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  about letting the yuan <CNY=> <CNY=NDF> <CNYCFXS=> appreciate in a free and open \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2 Then there's the Federal Reserve to consider. A deepening housing slump has forced the Fed to cut benchmark interest rates twice since September and more cuts may be on the way.   \par \par \uc2 That makes it tough for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly by purchasing dollars on behalf of the Treasury at a time when rate cuts are pushing down the value of the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 While Fed chief Ben Bernanke has conceded that the weak dollar could add to inflationary pressure, analysts say any sign of more credit-related turmoil at Wall Street banks would likely batter stock prices and force the Fed to cut again.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't think anyone in present circumstances would want the Fed to be constrained in what it could do," said Bernard Connolly, global strategist at Banque AIG in London. "Keeping the Fed from cutting rates if the U.S. economy needs it -- that's something Treasury must avoid at all costs."   \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in a similar predicament. With inflation still a concern, it has been reluctant to drop its monetary policy tightening bias, meaning any attempt to weaken the euro <EUR=> would likely fail.   \par \par \uc2 This was seen this year, albeit on a smaller scale, when the Reserve Bank of New Zealand tried but failed to stall rapid gains in the "kiwi" dollar <NZD=> while also raising rates.   \par \par \uc2 TALKING IT THROUGH   \par \par \uc2 While the dollar has been weakening since 2001, its slide has accelerated this year. After falling on average 3.2 percent on a trade-weighted basis, the dollar is down 6.3 percent so far this year, according to the Federal Reserve.   \par \par \uc2 Against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of seven major trading partners, the dollar had by November shed 12 percent year-on-year.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts now fear that dollar selling has grown so one-sided that it will take coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- or the threat of it -- to stem the slide.   \par \par \uc2 That leaves officials with the option of firing verbal volleys, hoping the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if not the real thing, will help soothe volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.   \par \par \uc2 Talk has certainly increased: ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet reiterated his aversion to "brutal" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge called the Canadian dollar's rally "abnormally quick" and Japanese Prime Minister Yasuo Fukuda said the yen was appreciating "too fast."   \par \par \uc2 Bank of England Governor Mervyn King also expressed growing concern with the unwinding of global imbalances, often considered shorthand for dollar depreciation.   \par \par \uc2 Even Paulson chimed in at an impromptu news conference, saying the U.S. economy's size, strength and openness explain why the dollar remains the world's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's Jen said officials are turning to jawboning not so much to reverse dollar losses as to stop them from going further, adding:   \par \par \uc2 "They don't have the determination to take on the market (with actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) at this point."   \par \par \uc2 However, if the dollar decline accelerates to the point where U.S. stock and bond prices plunge and foreigners lose the desire to hold them, the G7 may have to do more than talk.   \par \par \uc2 The last time the Group of Seven rich nations waded in to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets en masse was in 2000 to prop up the euro, which had lost 30 percent since its inception the year before.   \par \par \uc2 MacKinnon said even the U.S. Treasury won't stand by and allow a run on the dollar that destabilizes U.S. and global asset markets.   \par \par \uc2 "In extremis, and despite its free-market rhetoric, the U.S. Treasury does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has done before and that can't be ruled out, MacKinnon added.   \par \par \uc2 "The endgame is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. "We're in the early stages where repeated verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the norm. But verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically doesn't succeed." (Editing by James Dalgleish)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|UKI|Z|CANT|AUF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071115e3bf002r3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
