\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Canada says to press China on yuan at G20 meeting   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Louise Egan   \par \uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2007\par 17:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)   \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Canada sees an increased risk of a disorderly correction of global imbalances and would press hard at an upcoming G20 meeting for China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate more quickly, a finance official said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 But Ottawa appeared satisfied with continued dialogue on the issue, which it admits has yielded limited progress so far, falling short of demanding any specific measures by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to smooth out \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.   \par \par \uc2 The official was briefing reporters before this weekend's meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the Group of 20 nations in Cape Town, South Africa.   \par \par \uc2 Canada's new mantra at multilateral finance meetings is to argue that its economy has borne a disproportionate share of the burden from the correction of global imbalances, a term used to describe the gap between huge trade surpluses in Asia and the United States' gaping trade deficit.   \par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar has borne one-third of the adjustment to the U.S. dollar decline, the same portion as Europe but its economy ships three-quarters of all exports to the United States and is ten times smaller than the European Union.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has fallen sharply against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the past several months, while the Canadian dollar has soared, reaching parity with the U.S. dollar on Sept. 20 and further strengthening since then.   \par \par \uc2 The official said U.S. domestic demand was softening and its current account deficit was shrinking but still large, while China's current account surplus continued to grow.   \par \par \uc2 Ottawa is betting that China will be persuaded out of self-interest to loosen its grip on the yuan beyond the small steps it has already taken. Its accumulation of reserves and a large trade surplus are seen fueling domestic inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Chinese consumer inflation hit 6.2 percent in September, near a decade high, reinforcing expectations of further interest rate hikes and adding to worries that rising costs could undermine social stability.   \par \par \uc2 Canada at first took the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a source of pride, pointing to strong commodity prices and a vibrant economy to explain the rise.   \par \par \uc2 But, last week, Prime Minister Stephen Harper and Finance Minister Jim Flaherty began to express concern the Canadian dollar had risen too fast and could threaten economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 Flaherty has said the weak U.S. dollar needs to be addressed at the G20 meeting.   \par \par \uc2 The official would not speculate on Tuesday on whether the G20 would entertain any proposals for co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a notion that has resurfaced due to record-breaking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements around the world.   \par \par \uc2 The official also wouldn't comment on the chances for concrete action from China, which will be present at the meeting.   \par \par \uc2 Canada has no targeted level for its exchange rate and will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets in the most exceptional circumstances, the official said.   \par \par \uc2 Flaherty will discuss the slumping U.S. dollar in a bilateral meeting with U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson in South Africa.   \par \par \uc2 The official told reporters that the G20 would also discuss sovereign wealth funds -- pools of state-held investments. He said the funds could be useful vehicles to help manage windfall revenues but said many of them needed greater transparency and accountability.   \par \par \uc2 He said that all the countries around the table, including those with large sovereign wealth funds, had a growing appreciation of the need for standards for transparency. (Reporting by Louise Egan; Editing by Rob Wilson)   \par \par \uc2 CANADA-G20/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|Z|CANT|RBN|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071113e3bd001up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
