\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-ECB's Trichet warns on "brutal"  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  moves, holds rates   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andreas Framke   \par \uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2007\par 08:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Trichet comment on yuan)   \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT, Nov 8 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday that "brutal" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are not welcome but his vow to keep a grip on inflation gave a further boost to the soaring euro.   \par \par \uc2 The ECB kept interest rates on hold at 4 percent, as expected, but Trichet's firm stance on inflation and a downbeat growth assessment from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke pushed the euro back towards record highs above $1.47.   \par \par \uc2 Traders are betting on a narrowing rate gap between the United States and the euro zone, where the ECB faces opposing challenges from risks to growth coupled with rising inflation, while the Fed is expected to cut rates again.   \par \par \uc2 The euro has risen about 5 cents <EUR=> in the last two weeks, prompting fresh complaints from French President Nicholas Sarkozy. Trichet said it had become even clearer that a strong dollar was in the United States' interests.   \par \par \uc2 "In the recent period I have observed (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) moves that I would say were undoubtedly sharp and abrupt and I have said already that brutal moves are never welcome," Trichet told a news conference after the ECB left rates unchanged for the fifth month in a row.   \par \par \uc2 The use of the word "brutal" revives language he last used in late 2004, when the euro was around $1.29. But Trichet avoided a question on whether euro gains had already been brutal. He also declined comment on the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 In a later speech over dinner, Trichet called on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow the yuan to appreciate faster, saying an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  imposes disadvantages on exporters in other countries.   \par \par \uc2 "It is essential that China increasingly assumes the global responsibilities that inevitably accompany its growing economic clout," he said at a Frankfurt event on China-Europe relations.   \par \par \uc2 As well as breaking all records against the dollar, the euro is also at a record high on a trade-weighted basis <EUREER=ECBF>. Though the yuan has risen more than 10 percent against the U.S. dollar since 2005, against the euro it has weakened by 8 percent, Trichet noted.   \par \par \uc2 The ECB must tread a tightrope between avoiding fuelling rapid euro appreciation, which could hurt growth, and sounding tough on euro zone inflation which at 2.6 percent is well above the ECB's target and at its highest in over two years.   \par \par \uc2 "The sharp increase is a matter of particular concern," Trichet said after the rate meeting, adding that the ECB was determined to avoid knock-on effects which could shake loose inflation expectations and feed into a wage-price spiral.   \par \par \uc2 INFLATION WORSE, BUT TRANSITORY   \par \par \uc2 At the same time the ECB needed to gather more information on the impact of financial market turmoil before making further decisions on monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said Trichet's comments ended any lingering risk that the ECB might resume its tightening cycle as early as December. Before market turbulence hit in August the ECB had been seen raising rates to 4.5 percent this year. Most observers now expect a pause at 4 percent throughout 2008.   \par \par \uc2 "There is no indication of a near-term rate hike at all," Citibank economist Jose Alzola said.   \par \par \uc2 "At the same time, despite the downside risks to growth, the ECB insists that the medium-term outlook remains favourable so there is no reason to contemplate a rate cut either. The next rate move will depend on which risk, if any, eventually materialises."   \par \par \uc2 Trichet, who has just passed the half-way mark of his eight-year term, refused to give any hint of how long the rate pause might last, saying the ECB was constantly alert and reserved the right to act at any time.   \par \par \uc2 Trichet said the expected rise in inflation in coming months, due largely to base effects from oil, would be exacerbated by a steep rise in food and commodity prices, which were adding 0.2-0.3 percentage points to the headline rate.   \par \par \uc2 "The hump will be probably be a little bit longer and certainly bigger in terms of absolute magnitude than what we would have (seen otherwise)," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Euro zone economic growth was likely to continue around its potential rate, although financial market turbulence had increased uncertainty and downside risks, Trichet said.   \par \par \uc2 "Sustained real economic growth experienced in the euro area in the first half of 2007 has continued through the third quarter," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Italian employers' group Confindustria said on Thursday that risks to growth were increasing and European trade unions called on the ECB to cut interest rates to ward off a slowdown.   \par \par \uc2 Three-month interbank lending costs remain above 4.5 percent, pushing up corporate and household borrowing costs, three months after credit market turmoil sparked by the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis first hit Europe.   \par \par \uc2 In a sign that the ECB does not expect money market turmoil to be over soon, the bank said it would offer extra three-month funds later this month and in mid-December to replace existing extra funding that will be due for repayment.   \par \par \uc2 These higher borrowing costs, combined with uncertainty about export demand, have damaged industrial confidence as measured by euro zone purchasing managers' surveys.   \par \par \uc2 In September ECB staff forecast growth slowing to 2.3 percent next year -- still in line with the euro zone's 2.0-2.5 percent long-term trend -- from an estimated 2.5 percent for 2007. New staff forecasts will be issued next month (Additional reporting by Krista Hughes and David Milliken) (Writing by David Milliken and Krista Hughes, editing by Ian Jones, Richard Satran)   \par \par \uc2 ECB-RATES/ (UPDATE 5) (TV, PIX)|LANGEN|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|ABX|BNX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071108e3b80010m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
