\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Up Late; Ctrl Bank Suspected Of Supporting US Dlr   \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2007\par 10:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   7.5108               7.5157 \par USD/CNY OTC 0730 GMT     7.5117    +0.01%     7.5148 \par              High        7.5140    +0.04% \par              Low         7.5034    -0.10% \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--The euro's strength against the U.S. dollar boosted the Chinese yuan Friday, but traders suspected that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capped the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gains.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closed from Monday to Oct. 5 for the National Day holiday, dealers were wary of forecasting how the yuan would move when the market reopens Oct. 8, but most said they expect the dollar won't hold out against the CNY7.5000 level for much longer.   \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY7.5117 around 0730 GMT, down from Thursday's close of CNY7.5148. It traded between CNY7.5034 and CNY7.5140.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers had expected the yuan to gain strongly Friday after the dollar again dropped to an all-time low against the euro overnight.   \par \par \uc2 Thursday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.4148, up from $1.4127 late Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 But the yuan gave up some gains against the dollar after an early climb Friday.   \par \par \uc2 "When a firm order was placed on the automatic price matching system to buy dollars at CNY7.5100, the market suspected it was a signal from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a Shanghai-based dealer with a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2 Banks tend to use the OTC market more than the automatic price-matching system for trading because the transaction costs are lower.   \par \par \uc2 But unlike the OTC market, bids on the price-matching system are anonymous, leading some dealers to suspect that unusually large positions are taken by the People's  \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  to signal its preferences.   \par \par \uc2 Throughout the week dealers have suspected the PBOC of being active in containing the yuan's rise, particularly after the yuan rose strongly earlier in the month.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said Friday's trading was fairly light with the market winding down before the holiday.   \par \par \uc2 "A lot of dealers took today off and got an early start on the break," said a Shanghai-based dealer at a Japanese bank.   \par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards were at 7.0640/7.0690, down from 7.0660/7.0800 late Thursday afternoon.   \par \par \uc2 -By Denis McMahon, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon@dowjones.com [ 28-09-07 0824GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070928e39s000vb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
