atic price-matching system for trading because the transaction costs are lower. But unlike the OTC market, bids on the price-matching system are anonymous, leading some dealers to suspect that unusually large positions are taken by the People's  \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  to signal its preferences. Throughout the week dealers have suspected the PBOC of being active in containing the yuan's rise, particularly after the yuan rose strongly earlier in the month. Dealers said Friday's trading was fairly light with the market winding down before the holiday. 'A lot of dealers took today off and got an early start on the break,' said a Shanghai-based dealer at a Japanese bank. Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards were at 7.0640/7.0690, down from 7.0660/7.0800 late Thursday afternoon. -By Denis McMahon, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070928e39s00439\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
