\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-China needs a freer yuan, EU's Almunia says   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Wheatley, China Economics Editor   \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2007\par 09:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes from news conference)   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 18 (Reuters) - China should let the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move more freely to gain flexibility in conducting monetary policy and help to reduce global economic imbalances, EU Monetary Affairs Commissioner Joaquin Almunia said on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Almunia noted that China had promised to change its exchange rate regime gradually but he said progress had been rather slow.   \par \par \uc2 "A more vigorous approach to reform is needed to prevent the current account surplus from expanding further and to reduce the risks to the global economy," he said in a speech at Beijing's Tsinghua University,.   \par \par \uc2 The World Bank forecasts that China's current account surplus will reach 12 percent of gross domestic product this year, up from 9.5 percent in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 "In addition, a more flexible exchange rate policy would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates and curb investment -- in the long run, this would help China to rely less on exports and more on consumption and investment geared towards the domestic market," said Almunia.   \par \par \uc2 To hold down the yuan, the People's  \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  buys most of the dollars flowing into the economy from its huge trade surplus and investment inflows. It then sells bills to mop up the cash it has injected into the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 Almunia said the need to absorb so much money flowing into the economy made it difficult for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stave off inflationary pressure and economic overheating.   \par \par \uc2 China had roughly $1.4 trillion in reserves by the end of July, and Almunia said he had been told by Chinese officials that this was at least twice as much as they would consider "normal" given China's economic circumstances.   \par \par \uc2 Almunia said he had met Hu Xiaolian, head of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which currently manages all the reserves, and Lou Jiwei, who will run a sovereign wealth fund being set up to manage part of the reserves more aggressively.   \par \par \uc2 PAY ATTENTION   \par \par \uc2 The yuan, also known as the renminbi, has risen 7.76 percent against the dollar since it was revalued by 2.1 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July 2005 and depegged from the dollar to float within managed bands.   \par \par \uc2 But Almunia said the yuan had not risen against the euro over the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  period, and the euro zone's trade deficit with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rising sharply.   \par \par \uc2 He urged China to measure the yuan's appreciation against the inflation-adjusted exchange rates of all its trading partners -- and not just against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "The Chinese authorities should pay more attention to the real effective exchange rate of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not only to the bilateral exchange rate of the renminbi against some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Economists cite China's huge reserves and trade surplus, which jumped 71 percent in the first eight months to $161.76 billion, as evidence that the yuan is fundamentally undervalued.   \par \par \uc2 Besides a more flexible yuan, Almunia said Beijing needed further financial reforms, and to raise interest rates and bring about better-quality investment to trim the current account surplus and achieve better balance growth.   \par \par \uc2 "A concerted effort to tackle imbalances would also send a clear message to the protectionist forces that appear to be on the rise in various parts of the world," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Asked about the recent turmoil in global financial markets, Almunia said it was too early to give a precise answer.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy would take a hit over the rest of 2007 and in 2008. But he said the EU's central scenario was that the U.S. economy would keep growing at 1.5 to 2.0 percent, in which case the global impact would not be as great as some people think.   \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, he added, financial conditions had tightened and it was not clear how quickly investors and consumers regained confidence.   \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ALMUNIA/CHINA (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070918e39i000lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
