\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BIS-BIS's Knight urges tighter monetary policy   \b0\par\par\uc2 By David Milliken and Krista Hughes   \par \uc2 346 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2007\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Combines stories)   \par \par \uc2 BASEL, Switzerland, June 24 (Reuters) - Central banks should keep normalising interest rates to help tackle the highly accommodative financial conditions that lie at the heart of the main risks facing the world economy, the Bank for International Settlements' general manager said on Sunday.   \par \par \uc2 Malcolm Knight, speaking after a gathering of the world's top central bankers at the BIS in Basel, pointed to a resurgence of inflation, current account deficits and financial market vulnerabilities as key risks to future global prosperity.   \par \par \uc2 "Behind each set of concerns lurks the common factor of highly accommodative financial conditions. A further tightening of monetary policy might then be needed," he said in a statement released by the BIS.   \par \par \uc2 "Authorities should continue gradually to normalise the level of policy interest rates as long as domestic conditions allow. This would help to contain any inflationary risks," Knight continued in the text of a speech issued by the BIS.   \par \par \uc2 Tighter monetary policy would also help tackle financial market vulnerabilities linked to the underpricing of risk, Knight added.   \par \par \uc2 The BIS has no formal power, but top officials from the U.S. Federal Reserve, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank of Japan and People's Bank of China, as well as many others, attend its meetings.   \par \par \uc2 Knight also urged governments and central banks to allow markets to do more of the work in setting exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2 "Countries that in principle have floating exchange rate regimes should allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adjust more freely," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "Such normalisation would also mean allowing the exchange rate to do more of the work in countries where the authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily," he added.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been regularly urged by major Western economies to allow greater flexibility for the yuan, which they believe to be undervalued and giving an unfair boost to Chinese exports. (Additional reporting by Sven Egenter)   \par \par \uc2 BIS/KNIGHT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|UKI|J|Z|NAT|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070624e36o000c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
