\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-China cool to IMF policy shift on  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  surveillance   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Wheatley, China Economics Editor   \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2007\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments by government researcher, paragraphs 21-22)   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, June 20 (Reuters) - China reacted coolly on Wednesday to a change in the International Monetary Fund's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  monitoring policies that has been widely interpreted as increasing pressure on Beijing to let the yuan rise faster.   \par \par \uc2 The new rules stipulate that member countries, as well as avoiding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should avoid policies that result in external instability.   \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has expressed reservations about the adoption of this decision as it does not fully reflect the developing countries' opinions," the People's Bank of China said on its Web site,   {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn  }}}\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director Rodrigo Rato has said the revision was not aimed at any one country, but IMF board sources in Washington told Reuters that China, Egypt and Iran did not support the policy shift announced on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The changes come amid rising tension over China's record trade surplus with the United States, which Washington blames in part on what it says is an undervalued yuan <CNY=CFXS>.   \par \par \uc2 Experience had shown that exchange rate adjustments had a certain role to play in resolving external imbalances, but they were not the ultimate or only policy instrument, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a rare English-language statement.   \par \par \uc2 "Large and disorderly exchange rate adjustments will not only exacerbate external instability, but also affect the sustainability of a country's domestic economic growth, and subsequently the sustainable growth of the global economy and stability of international financial markets," the PBOC said.   \par \par \uc2 Economists interpreted the firmly worded statement as a reaffirmation of China's intention to keep letting the yuan rise, but at a pace of its choosing.   \par \par \uc2 "The underlying fear is that it goes a bit too far, too fast," said Adrian Foster, a strategist with Dresdner Kleinwort in Beijing.   \par \par \uc2 But he said Beijing would want to fly under the radar in the run-up to the November 2008 U.S. presidential election. To that end, China was likely to try to head off criticism by pushing up the yuan to around 7 per dollar by next spring, Foster said.   \par \par \uc2 TOO TOUGH TO FORECAST   \par \par \uc2 The yuan was trading around 7.6170 to the dollar on Wednesday, a further gain of 6.5 percent since it was revalued by 2.1 percent against the dollar in July 2005 and untethered from a dollar peg to float within managed bands.   \par \par \uc2 While the U.S. Treasury declined last week to rule that Beijing is deliberately keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued, U.S. lawmakers say China gives its exporters an unfair price advantage in global markets by preventing the yuan from rising faster.   \par \par \uc2 Government economist Li Yang last year correctly forecast the yuan's end-2006 rate, but on Wednesday he said there were too many uncertainties to guess where it would be at the end of 2007.   \par \par \uc2 "This year is quite different from last year," Li, a researcher with the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences and a former adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told a forum.   \par \par \uc2 "Pressure from the U.S. side on the exchange rate issue is getting stronger. It's a serious issue," Li added.   \par \par \uc2 In its statement, the PBOC pushed back.   \par \par \uc2 Without mentioning the United States by name, it urged the IMF to "strengthen policy surveillance over those members who issue major reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and thereby have a major impact on global financial stability.   \par \par \uc2 The revised IMF rules are the first changes to the IMF's legal framework on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surveillance since 1977. They aim to reflect sweeping changes in the global economy since then, including the rapid ascent of China and India.   \par \par \uc2 But China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that, in assessing the impact of globalisation, the IMF must examine the role of exchange rates objectively as one factor among many.   \par \par \uc2 "In the past years, many developing countries, China included, have worked hard to adjust their domestic economic structure, improve market mechanisms and increase exchange rate flexibility. These efforts have contributed to rapid growth of the global economy," it said.   \par \par \uc2 However, Zhang Liqun, a researcher with the Development Research Centre, a think-tank under the State Council, China's cabinet, said a stronger exchange rate was the key to tackling the root cause of China's record trade surplus.   \par \par \uc2 "An adjustment of the yuan's rate is the fundamental way to balance the external and internal sectors of the economy," Zhang said in a Web cast on Wednesday. (Additional reporting by Zhou Xin)   \par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|U|NAT|J|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070620e36k0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
