\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  won't directly  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in stock market -adviser   \b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2007\par 03:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Tuesday morning stock market move)   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, May 22 (Reuters) - The Chinese government is unlikely to interfere directly in the domestic stock market, a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser was quoted on Tuesday as saying.   \par \par \uc2 Some securities analysts and fund managers have said Beijing might take stronger measures to rein in the current bull run in the stock market and drive out speculative money.   \par \par \uc2 The Securities Times quoted Fan Gang as saying the latest monetary tightening moves were largely aimed at reducing money supply and excess liquidity in the economy rather than at the stock market itself.   \par \par \uc2 "The government is not going to take steps to support or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. The market will be left alone to undertake its own risks," said Fan,a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee.   \par \par \uc2 "The market is getting mature and so is the government."   \par \par \uc2 China's stock market shrugged off the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on Friday to raise bank interest rates and reserve requirements, ending up 1 percent on Monday. It rose further on Tuesday, with the benchmark stock index <.SSEC> gaining 1.51 percent by 0255 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 There had been concerns more market-cooling steps may be on the cards.   \par \par \uc2 In 1996, the government halted a bull run in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  A share market by using the People's Daily, the Communist Party's mouthpiece, to warn in an editorial against "excessive speculation".   \par \par \uc2 Early this month, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Zhou Xiaochuan said he was concerned by a stock bubble and would monitor asset prices.   \par \par \uc2 Fan was quoted as saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  always kept a close eye on asset prices when it came to drafting monetary policies because fluctuations in asset prices will lead to fluctuations in the economy, a key lesson of the 1997/98 Asian economic crisis.   \par \par \uc2 China's inflationary pressure was easing due to softening grain prices, but the possibility of a sharp rebound could not be ruled out yet, Fan said.   \par \par \uc2 CHINA-STOCKS/ECONOMIST (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070522e35m0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
