\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Paulson: China Must Show Quick Progress On Reform   \b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2007\par 20:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (adds background) \par  \par    By Elizabeth Price \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOSTON (Dow Jones)--U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson Thursday said China needs to show progress in implementing its reform agenda in the short term, to ensure both the U.S. and China continue to benefit from their trading relationship.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Paulson, in remarks to students at the Harvard Business School, said the U.S. and China have mutually benefitted from trade with each other. Together, they have accounted for half of world economic growth over the past five years. However, as the volume of trade has grown, trade tensions have as well. Defusing those tensions through dialogue will require Chinese action in the short term, including reforming its managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, Paulson said.   \par \par \uc2 "We need to set priorities and we need to measure our progress in meeting those priorities and to do that we need to meet some short-term goals," Paulson said.   \par \par \uc2 Later this month, Paulson will host a delegation of senior Chinese officials for a second meeting of a Strategic Economic Dialogue between the two countries. Paulson said the dialogue is focussed on long-term issues, but near-term accomplishments can act as "signposts" along the way.   \par \par \uc2 Paulson is under pressure to get Chinese action from members of Congress threatening to enact legislation restricting imports from China in retaliation for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. He joked that "short-termism" on Wall Street is nothing compared to the focus on the short-run in Washington.   \par \par \uc2 China tightly manages the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a system of capital controls and market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Though \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accelerated the rate of yuan appreciation allowed against the U.S. dollar, the yuan has actually depreciated slightly on a trade-weighted basis as the value of the dollar has weakened in international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.   \par \par \uc2 In recent months the magnitude of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  required to control yuan appreciation has risen, evidenced by a jump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the first quarter this year.   \par \par \uc2 Paulson said China needs to move more rapidly on a path toward a market-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet its goals for shifting the composition of its growth toward domestic consumption and away from exports. It is also a crucial tool for China to develop its financial markets, Paulson said.   \par \par \uc2 "Over the last year, despite the goal they have of having domestic demand be a bigger portion of growth, it looks like to me it's been a smaller portion," Paulson said. "There is no country as big or as economically important as China that doesn't have a market determined \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - it's not sustainable longer term."   \par \par \uc2 China isn't going to develop the financial markets they want unless they have market signals derived from a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is market driven, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Further, the Chinese yuan is a political symbol for China's overall reform agenda, Paulson said, adding that meeting the liberalization commitments China made to join the World Trade Organization should only be a beginning for them.   \par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform alone won't eliminate China's trade surplus, Paulson said. That is a result of structural factors like China's high savings rate, he said. That is why the Bush administration's dialogue with China stresses financial market development and development of a social safety net in China to reduce the incentives for saving there.   \par \par \uc2 Paulson said U.S. exports to China have grown rapidly and should continue to do so.   \par \par \uc2 "I believe we will continue to see our exports to China growing rapidly for some years to come," Paulson said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com [ 03-05-07 1836GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070503e353000zx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
