\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE:China Raises Reserve Ratio;More Tightening Needed   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2007\par 09:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more comments; adds more background)   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sunday took another measure to soak up strong capital inflows into its red-hot economy, ratcheting up banks' reserve requirements for the seventh time in the past year.   \par \par \uc2 The move, which takes effect May 15, will bring the ratio up 0.5 percentage point to 11% for most banks and comes on the heels of an acceleration of economic growth to 11.1% in the first quarter.   \par \par \uc2 Economists widely expected a tightening move ahead of the upcoming May holiday and are now looking for the People's Bank of China to deploy more tightening measures, such as an interest rate hike, in the coming weeks to contain rising inflationary pressures and slow growth from the first quarter's blistering pace.   \par \par \uc2 The reserve ratio increase, because it signals more measures ahead, could cause traders to push domestic shares lower in trading Monday - the last day China's markets are open before a weeklong holiday.   \par \par \uc2 "The People's Bank tightened at this juncture to give the markets a little time to digest this increase," said Jing Ulrich, chairwoman of China equities at  \uc2 JP Morgan & Co.\uc2  "This one hike alone won't trigger a major sell-off."   \par \par \uc2 In a statement posted on its Web site Sunday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the reserve ratio increase will "strengthen the management of liquidity in the banking system and guide the reasonable growth of credit."   \par \par \uc2 Behind flush liquidity in the domestic economy are strong capital inflows that result from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, from appreciating. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $135.7 billion in the first quarter - more than half the increase for all of last year. That raised the total to a staggering $1.2 trillion by the end of March.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pays out domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to banks to buy their dollars, pumping liquidity into the Chinese economy. A reserve ratio increase forces banks to store more yuan at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning they have less funds to lend out to new investment projects.   \par \par \uc2 The acceleration in China's growth, driven chiefly by a strong trade surplus and bank lending to new investment projects, has forced Beijing to pull a variety of levers since last year, such as interest rates, reserve ratio increases and administrative measures, to cool sectors like property and steel that threaten to push prices higher.   \par \par \uc2 China's consumer price index rose 3.3% in March and 2.7% on year in the first quarter. That pickup has sparked concerns that after muted inflation in recent years, rising capacity might no longer be able to counterbalance demand and a surge in prices could be ahead for the world's fastest-growing major economy.   \par \par \uc2 After strong data in the first couple months of the year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accelerated its tightening. The last reserve requirement ratio increase took effect April 16, a month after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised benchmark deposit and lending rates by 27 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the moves, China's gross domestic product grew a stronger-than-expected 11.1% from a year ago in the first three months of this year, a pace Beijing sees as unsustainable and that exceeds the 10.7% annual rate of 2006.   \par \par \uc2 "We expect China's reserve ratio will likely rise to 12%-13% by the end of this year, in an aim to curb the liquidity caused by \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve and trade surplus growth," said BNP Paribas economist Isaac Meng in Beijing, who also is anticipating another interest rate hike around mid-year.   \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said in a note: "The past two years of experience in China has shown that (reserve requirement ratio) changes are an ineffective policy tool to control monetary expansion with the excess reserve ratio likely standing at about 2% (or more)."   \par \par \uc2 The investment house said ratio increases aren't binding on banks' capabilities to lend and recommended an interest rate adjustment as "a more credible" tightening measure.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Inflation, Investment Key Going Forward \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the short term, the reserve ratio increase will hurt market confidence as concerns about interest rate hikes linger in the stock market, said Zhu Haibin, an analyst at Everbright Securities. He said bank stocks will continue to drop Monday after a correction Friday, in part also due to their overpriced levels.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks both A and B shares, ended down 0.6% at 3759.87 Friday, after hitting record highs this month having gained 31% since the beginning of March.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond prices may be weak because the reserve ratio hike is expected to siphon around 160 billion yuan from the money market, said Wang Yingfeng, an analyst at Shanghai Securities. He said bond losses should be limited because some unsubscribed funds from recent initial public offerings should be returning to the market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's key seven-year bond yielded 3.30% Friday, compared with 3.44% in the previous session.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The massive inflows have fed into domestic liquidity, helping to accelerate first-quarter urban fixed-asset investment growth to 25.3% from a year earlier, compared with 2006's pace of 24.5%. In March, China's consumer price index rose 3.3% from a year ago, above the government's target of under 3% for 2007.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the inflation rate continues to climb, if the momentum of fixed-asset investment growth doesn't slow down, and if the asset prices rise too quickly, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  definitely will tighten further," said Yu Yongding, a former academic member of the monetary policy committee of the People's Bank of China.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yu said possible moves include interest rate hikes, higher reserve requirements and more bill issuances by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to mop up liquidity.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank said the latest move by the PBOC signals Beijing isn't likely to use \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy to absorb flush liquidity and reflects the government's intention to use interest rates as a key role in reining in economic growth.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rick Carew and J.R. Wu; Victoria Ruan and Rose Yu contributed to this story, Dow Jones Newswires; 86-10-6588-5848; jr.wu@dowjones.com [ 29-04-07 0721GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070429e34t00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
