\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Malaysian ringgit, Sing dlr at multi-year highs  \b0\par\par\uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 03:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 21 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit and Singapore dollar hit multi-year highs on Wednesday, with most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firm as the U.S. dollar took a knock after a report that China would stop amassing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit received an additional boost as Malaysia's prime minister said on Tuesday that Malaysia wanted a firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Further signs of stabilisation in global stock markets after this month's volatility added to the bullish tone in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The China story is one factor," said BNP Paribas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Thio Chin Loo. "Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are higher and that shows risk aversion has abated a bit. We are looking at calmer markets."  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar <AUD=>, which hit a 10-year high versus the U.S. dollar on Wednesday, and the New Zealand dollar have risen sharply this week, a sign that risk appetite is picking up again, dealers say.  \par \par \uc2 In Asia, high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> and Philippine peso <PHP=> have also rebounded.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> hit a one-month high at about 936 per dollar, while the rupiah gained as much as 0.6 percent to a three-week peak at 9,120 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 China will stop stockpiling foreign reserves, its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Zhou Xiaochuan, said in an interview published on Tuesday. The comments hurt the U.S. dollar in overnight trade although analysts questioned whether China would in practice be able to stop accumulating reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose as far as 1.5240 to the U.S. dollar <SGD=>, its highest level since October 1997. In late trade on Tuesday it was quoted at 1.5255.  \par \par \uc2 The rise, however, made operators wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  story overnight and selling of the U.S. dollar is driving the Sing dollar," said one trader in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't see them (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in the market, but there were rumours that they were there earlier. If they were there, dollar/Sing would not have come down so fast."  \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee <INR=IN> hit a 19-month high at about 43.61 per dollar as traders sold dollars to raise funds to cope with a cash shortage that pushed interbank rates to their highest level in more than 10 years.  \par \par \uc2 RINGGIT SHINES  \par \par \uc2 The ringgit rose 0.6 percent to around 3.4720 per dollar -- its strongest in nine years.  \par \par \uc2 It gained momentum after Malaysian Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi said on Tuesday that exporters would be able to cope with a slow, steady appreciation in the ringgit.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters and CNBC, Abdullah said he welcomed a strong ringgit as a vote of investor confidence.  \par \par \uc2 "The Badawi comments are the key reason for the ringgit gains," said ABN AMRO \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Shahab Jalinoos.  \par \par \uc2 He said the comments were important since they contrasted sharply with the usual perception of Asian policy makers as being in favour of a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support exporters.  \par \par \uc2 A trader at Hypo und Vereinsbank said the rupiah was bolstered by expectations of further Indonesian interest rate cuts that were boosting the local bond market.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Indonesia sold 9.2 trillion rupiah ($1 billion) in 10 and 15-year bonds, against an indicative target of 3 trillion rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 "The rupiah is stronger because there are indications of further rate cuts, which have boosted offshore demand for bonds," the trader said. "The market talk is that 60-65 percent of the bonds sold yesterday were taken up by offshore."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0750 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.51         117.71       +0.17\par  Sing dlr        1.5241         1.5256       +0.10\par  Taiwan dlr      33.102         33.125       +0.07\par  Korean won      938.20         941.40       +0.34\par  Baht             34.77          34.82       +0.14\par  Peso             48.42          48.59       +0.35\par  Rupiah         9142.00        9172.00       +0.33\par  Rupee            43.65          43.86       +0.49\par  Ringgit         3.4745         3.4940       +0.56\par  Yuan            7.7344         7.7370       +0.03\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.51         119.01       +1.28\par  Sing dlr        1.5241         1.5337       +0.63\par  Taiwan dlr      33.102         32.585       -1.56\par  Korean won      938.20        0929.50       -0.93\par  Baht             34.75          36.08       +3.83\par  Peso             48.42          49.03       +1.27\par  Rupiah         9142.00        8988.00       -1.68\par  Rupee            43.65          44.26       +1.40\par  Ringgit         3.4745         3.5270       +1.51\par  Yuan