\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 6-China raises banks' required reserves  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2007\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Feb 16 (Reuters) - China on Friday raised banks' required reserves for the fifth time since June in an attempt to keep a check on credit expansion in the world's fourth-largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing is struggling to mop up cash gushing into the banking system from China's big balance-of-payments surplus, which critics say is rooted in an unfairly low exchange rate for the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:  \par \par \uc2 WANG QING, ECONOMIST AT BANK OF AMERICA IN HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "We expect China to raise bank reserve requirements by a total of 200 basis points this year, so there is the scope of rising the requirements by 150 basis points after the latest increase.  \par \par \uc2 "Given that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are likely to rise by about $250 billion this year, and at the same time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep annual money supply growth at 16 percent, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to raise reserve requirements to reach that target.  \par \par \uc2 "The latest official data showed that money supply growth slowed in January due partly to the new year effect, but we cannot see any slowdown in bank credit expansion.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the chances of raising interest rates still exist. If inflation exceeds or is close to 3 percent in February, then the chance of raising interest rates would be fairly big in March or April."  \par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN SACHS, IN A NOTE TO CLIENTS:  \par \par \uc2 "In our view, this hike has delivered a strong tightening signal right before the Chinese New Year. This surprise move by the PBOC could have been triggered by the strong credit growth in January.  \par \par \uc2 "This 50 basis-point hike ... highlights the challenges facing the PBOC to conduct monetary policy under a very open economy and a significantly undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We maintain our view that the RRR change is a blunt and inefficient tool for conducting monetary policy. The past 4 years' experiences in China have shown that its effectiveness tends to erode very quickly by continued inflows in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  driven by the undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "In addition, with the excess reserve ratio likely standing at about 3-4 percent, the 50 basis-point increase in the RRR is not binding on banks' capability to lend.  \par \par \uc2 "In the short run, the reserve requirement hike will likely push up market interest rates and the yield curve modestly in China. However, we do not expect this tightening measure to have much impact on the real economy or the financial markets."  \par \par \uc2 FRANK GONG, HEAD OF CHINA RESEARCH AND CHIEF ECONOMIST AT JPMORGAN IN HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "We expect them to raise the reserve requirement ratio, maybe three times more down the road until it reaches 11 percent -- because the bank requirement ratio is the best monetary policy tool for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment given the current liquidity and the macro condition.  \par \par \uc2 "The reserve requirement hike will simply prevent more asset liquidity (going) into the economy, but it would not actually worsen the liquidity in the system."  \par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS KWAN, ASIA/PACIFIC ECONOMIST, STANDARD CHARTERED BANK IN HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "This is a good move in the sense that they need to keep tightening because there is still too much liquidity. The reserve requirement has been the tool for this, and this may not be the last increase.  \par \par \uc2 "They'll probably take a break now after two increases this year. They need time to see the results and probably in the second quarter they'll see if they have to do more. There are signs that liquidity is easing a bit. We believe they will also probably raise interest rates again, but this is more difficult to manage."  \par \par \uc2 JAN LAMBREGTS, ECONOMIST WITH RABOBANK INTERNATIONAL, HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "The huge trade surplus and steady foreign direct investments are driving inflows. Because China is running a crawling peg against the dollar in practice, it needs to buy a lot of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flowing into the country as a result of these inflows.  \par \par \uc2 "You can't sterilise it all -- the bond markets are not deep enough. This move effectively taxes the banking system and curbs the extent to which banks can lend.  \par \par \uc2 "It may not be the best solution, but in the short term it is one which seems to work for China. This is also not the last increase that we are going to see.  \par \par \uc2 "It will curb loan growth to some extent, but we should not exaggerate the extent to which it will work."  \par \par \uc2 KENT YAU, ECONOMIST WITH CORE PACIFIC-YAMAICHI IN HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "I think that going forward they're very serious about tightening liquidity, owing to the fact that liquidity sneaking into the stock market is pretty dangerous.  \par \par \uc2 "In the past 10 years, the Chinese market has always been up and down -- and mostly down too. It's going to hurt the small investors. It would get even worse if the market goes up more, because the burst would be even more spectacular.  \par \par \uc2 "The tightening of liquidity looks even more urgent, because in the past it was more on the macro side. The macro side takes a little time, so there's some lag effect. But on the stock market side, they definitely want to see some more immediate action before it gets out of hand."  \par \par \uc2 BEN SIMPFENDORFER, STRATEGIST, ROYAL BANK OF SCOTLAND, HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "No real surprise. Again, this is all about removing liquidity rather than hiking rates.  \par \par \uc2 "It signals concern on the size of the trade surplus, and asset price inflation. It does argue for steady CNY appreciation of near 5 percent, annually.  \par \par \uc2 "We expect at least one more hike in the reserve ratio in this half."  \par \par \uc2 RICHARD YETSENGA, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, HSBC, HONG KONG:  \par \par \uc2 "It is indicative of a shift in China to other avenues to try and sterilise the liquidity growth that has resulted from \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The fundamental problem hasn't changed. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to get about $15 billion in new domestic credit creation every month, which is far too much.  \par \par \uc2 "But the sterilisation task will persist and continue to pressure the policy process." SEAN CALLOW, \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, WESTPAC BANK, SINGAPORE:  \par \par \uc2 "This move has come a little earlier than we had expected but is not likely to be the end of the tightening phase.  \par \par \uc2 "We see at least another two 50 basis points hikes in the required reserve ratio to 11 percent by year end.  \par \par \uc2 "It helps take some of the pressure out of sterilising \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows through bill issuance. We do see a modest rise in interest rates as well, to 6.39 percent by mid-year from 6.12 percent."  \par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:  \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> was trading at 7.7465 per dollar at 0906 GMT, compared with 7.7479 before the move.  \par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/REQUIREMENTS VIEW (UPDATE 5)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|Z|ABX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070219e32g000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
