\par\par \b \uc2 DJ CEA: China Yuan Policy May Not Change Its Export Prices  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Henry J. Pulizzi and Elizabeth Price Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2007\par 07:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--China's policy of depressing the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushes domestic inflation higher, and leaves the relative price of goods the country trades unchanged, the White House said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 'Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not systematically change the relative real prices between the United States and China,' the Council of Economic Advisers said in its annual economic report of the president.  \par \par \uc2 China has been under heavy pressure from Congress and manufacturers to ease its tight grip on the yuan, which critics say undervalues the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gives Chinese products an unfair advantage in global markets. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets each month to control the value of the yuan, also known as the renminbi.  \par \par \uc2 According to Monday's report, the increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation rate, spurred by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , offsets any price declines resulting from the exchange rate movements. That conclusion suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have little impact on the U.S.'s trade balance with China, and raises the question of why the U.S. Treasury is devoting so much of its time and energy to persuading Beijing to allow the yuan to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 'Fixing the nominal exchange rate does not tend to move countries away from purchasing power parity,' the idea that identical goods and services should cost the same in different countries, CEA said. 'The only effect is that domestic goods prices have to do all of the adjustment since the exchange rate is fixed.'  \par \par \uc2 CEA said there is no 'clear preference' between conducting monetary policy with a fixed exchange rate or through short-term interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 'In the end, central banks that choose to fix the value of their exchange rate relative to another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and central banks that choose to set a short-term interest rate are each choosing a different tool to conduct monetary policy,' CEA said.  \par \par \uc2 In July 2005, China officially ended its policy of pegging its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, moving to a system of managing the yuan against an unpublished basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Though the yuan has appreciated more than 6% since July 2005, U.S. exporters, lawmakers and the Bush administration believe it needs to rise further against the dollar. The Treasury Department is pushing China to let the yuan appreciate in the short term, and move to a freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the longer run. The disagreement between Washington and Beijing is over the pace of reform.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, the White House's point man on the issue, told lawmakers last month that Beijing needs to take four steps toward floating the yuan. It must widen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band, lower \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of dollars, develop a bond market and create \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy targets to avoid inflation and boost confidence in the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 'China does not yet have the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy we want it to have and that it needs,' Paulson told the Senate Banking Committee. 'China's cautious approach to exchange rate reform exacerbates distortions in its domestic economy and impedes the adjustment of international imbalances.'  \par \par \uc2 Finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized countries, meeting over the weekend in Germany, urged China to focus on the trade-weighted value of the yuan. 'In emerging economies with large and growing current account surpluses, especially China, it is desirable that their effective exchange rates move so that necessary adjustments will occur,' the finance ministers said.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Monday, the Chinese government said the country's trade surplus jumped 67% last month from a year earlier to $15.88 billion. -By Henry J. Pulizzi, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9256; henry.pulizzi@dowjones.com; and Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202 862-9295; elizabeth.price@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070213e32d002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
