\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE:CEA:China Yuan Policy May Not Change Its Export Prices  \b0\par\par\uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2007\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2007, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Feb 12, 2007 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with further detail.)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Henry J. Pulizzi and Elizabeth Price\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's policy of depressing the value of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pushes domestic inflation higher, and leaves the relative price of goods the country trades unchanged, the White House said Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not systematically change the relative real prices between the United States and China," the Council of Economic Advisers said in its annual economic report of the president.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has been under heavy pressure from Congress and manufacturers to ease its tight grip on the yuan, which critics say undervalues the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and gives Chinese products an unfair advantage in global markets. China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys dollars on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets each month to control the value of the yuan, also known as the renminbi.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to Monday's report, the increase in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's inflation rate, spurred by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , offsets any price declines resulting from the exchange rate movements. That conclusion suggests the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have little impact on the U.S. trade balance with China, and raises the question of why the U.S. Treasury is devoting so much of its time and energy to persuading Beijing to allow the yuan to appreciate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Fixing the nominal exchange rate does not tend to move countries away from purchasing power parity," the idea that identical goods and services should cost the same in different countries, CEA said. "The only effect is that domestic goods prices have to do all of the adjustment since the exchange rate is fixed."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CEA said there is no "clear preference" between conducting monetary policy with a fixed exchange rate or through short-term interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the end, central banks that choose to fix the value of their exchange rate relative to another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and central banks that choose to set a short-term interest rate are each choosing a different tool to conduct monetary policy," CEA said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though the panel said economic theory doesn't dictate a preference between the two systems, it said interest-rate targeting is the preferred tool because no major industrialized nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  uses a fixed exchange rate. CEA Chairman Edward Lazear said the panel is "agnostic" on the issue.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our goal in this chapter was simply to explain the effects of a variety of different strategies, and that's why we said we don't want to take a position on one over the other," Lazear said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In July 2005, China officially ended its policy of pegging its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the dollar, moving to a system of managing the yuan against an unpublished basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though the yuan has appreciated more than 6% since July 2005, U.S. exporters, lawmakers and the Bush administration believe it needs to rise further against the dollar. The Treasury Department is pushing China to let the yuan appreciate in the short term, and move to a freely floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime in the longer run. The disagreement between Washington and Beijing is over the pace of reform.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, the White House's point man on the issue, told lawmakers last month that Beijing needs to take four steps toward floating the yuan. It must widen the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trading band, lower \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases of dollars, develop a bond market and create \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy targets to avoid inflation and boost confidence in the yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "China does not yet have the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy we want it to have and that it needs," Paulson told the Senate Banking Committee. "China's cautious approach to exchange rate reform exacerbates distortions in its domestic economy and impedes the adjustment of international imbalances."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized countries, meeting over the weekend in Germany, urged China to focus on the trade-weighted value of the yuan. "In emerging economies with large and growing current account surpluses, especially China, it is desirable that their effective exchange rates move so that necessary adjustments will occur," the finance ministers said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Monday, the Chinese government said the country's trade surplus jumped 67% last month from a year earlier to $15.88 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Henry J. Pulizzi and Elizabeth 