\par\par \b \uc2 TEXT-Treasury's Paulson's Senate Banking testimony  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2007\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Jan 31 (Reuters) - Following is the full text of Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson's prepared testimony delivered on Wednesday before a Senate Banking Committee hearing on International Economic and Exchange Rate Policy (IEERP) and the U.S.-China Strategic Economic Dialogue: "Mr. Chairman, Senator Shelby, and members of the committee, thank you for the opportunity to have this dialogue with you today on an issue of vital importance to American workers and the American economy. As you know, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report recently issued by the Treasury reviews developments in international economics and the exchange rate policies of a number of our key trading partners. Let me first take a few minutes to talk about the important and multi-faceted relationship we have with China. Getting it right is vitally important to the citizens of both our nations and the world and will be for many years to come.  \par \par \uc2  Since the economic relationship between our two countries is an important part of the overall relationship, I have focused intensely on China from the day I was confirmed. It is my job to press for opportunities for American businesses and American workers. The successful management of our economic relationship with China will benefit the United States, and China, greatly. The United States and China share many strategic interests. These range from national security, to economic growth and trade, to the health of our environment. As a growing leader on the world stage, China must be a full participant in the rules-based world economy. Recognizing this, the President and Chinese President Hu established the Strategic Economic Dialogue (SED) to manage the economic relationship between our two nations on a long-term basis. The SED should help us make progress on fundamental long-term structural economic issues, as well as on very pressing short-term issues. It is not a scripted ceremony. It is a serious, focused discussion of the economic issues that matter most. The SED provides a mechanism through which, for the first time in our relationship, our government can speak with a single voice on economic issues to the highest levels of the Chinese government, on a regular basis. The Dialogue is goals-based and designed to keep both sides moving forward on the goals we establish. By meeting regularly, we can actively monitor the progress we're making. By making progress on critical, immediate issues such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform, we will build confidence to deal with important longer term economic issues such as the structural challenges China faces. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is a key factor in our economic relationship. China does not yet have the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy we want it to have and that it needs. Treasury's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  report clearly states that China's cautious approach to exchange rate reform exacerbates distortions in its domestic economy and impedes the adjustment of international imbalances. I look forward to discussing the report with you during this hearing. We are actively pressing the Chinese to introduce greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility and undertake wider market reform. We are seeing some results. China abandoned its pegged exchange rate in July 2005, and began to introduce some flexibility. Since last July, the pace of appreciation has been more than three times as fast as it had been in the first year after the initial renminbi reform. Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, once conducted entirely by the Chinese government, is now conducted almost entirely by commercial banks. China has introduced financial instruments to hedge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. And the Chinese government has begun to allow increased fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is welcome progress, but we need to see much more. Although China is moving faster, it is still not moving fast enough. Nor is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility enough. A major objective of my two remaining years as Treasury Secretary will be pressing the Chinese government to advance toward the goal of a renminbi whose value is freely set in a competitive marketplace, based upon economic fundamentals. I will work with the Chinese government to develop the market infrastructure they need for a freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This involves several key steps. First, the government should progressively widen the band that limits the daily movement of the exchange rate. Widening the band will help businesses and financial institutions learn to operate with a fluctuating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Second, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should progressively reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Third, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  must develop the fundamental components of a capital market a bond market and a yield curve to absorb inflows and outflows of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and provide ways to hedge against exchange risk. And fourth, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must set clear policy targets to avoid inflation and thereby provide confidence in the value of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I want to be clear: Increased flexibility in the short run is absolutely necessary, but it is not sufficient. My goal is to make significant progress toward a fully market-determined, floating Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The message I delivered to Chinese decision-makers in the first meeting of our Strategic Economic Dialogue in December is that they are not moving quickly enough to make their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible. While they agree they need to introduce \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility and move to a floating exchange rate, they are not moving quickly enough for the United States or the rest of the global community. And they are not moving quickly enough for their own good. The Chinese leaders believe there is risk in moving too quickly, when in fact, as I argued to them, the greater risk is in moving too slowly. China may in some respects be a developing country, but it is also a large and powerful country. The international community will run out of patience with China unless the pace of its reform accelerates. [20070131 151940 GMT]  \par \par \uc2 Reform of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is a crucial issue for China and for the United States. And, Mr. Chairman, the need for reform in the Chinese economy goes beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement alone will not eliminate the distortions in the Chinese economy nor significantly reduce China's trade surplus. China needs to restructure its economy so that household consumption rather than exports and excess investment powers growth. This is the only way China can grow without generating huge trade surpluses. To do this, Chinese policy must address the reasons why Chinese households feel compelled to save so much and spend so little. Only 20 percent of the 800 million people who live in rural areas have health insurance. The basic government pension covered only 17 percent of Chinese workers in 2005. And only 14 percent of the population is covered by unemployment insurance. China must invest in its people by strengthening the health care system and the social safety net. And Chinese households need financial products that insure against risk and finance major expenditures. The Strategic Economic Dialogue addresses all of these issues. I believe that the openness of the U.S. economy to competition and our participation in international trade are key to economic growth, higher wages, and increased opportunities for U.S. workers. And we are pressing China to follow our example of openness. I am working to ensure that China's growth and expanding market create maximum opportunities for the United States. China must live up to its WTO commitments. It must protect and vigorously enforce intellectual property rights. It must increasingly open its markets to foreign competition -- or its own good as well as for ours. And it must introduce greater transparency in regulation and observe the rule of law. Through the Strategic Economic Dialogue, and through the various economic dialogues that we have with China, the Administration will continue to press very hard in all of these areas. Mr. Chairman, America's economy and workers benefit significantly from our trade with China. China is our fourth-largest export market. Our exports to China have increased more than 350 percent over the last decade six times the growth of our exports to the rest of the world. And nearly half of our exports to China are capital goods, including high-value-added goods such as civilian aircraft, electrical machinery, and medical devices. I believe strongly that a healthy Chinese economy, growing without large external imbalances, is of vital interest to the people of the United States, to the people of China, and to the global economy as a whole. More \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in the short term and a fully market-determined, floating RMB in the intermediate term are essential to accomplish that goal. So is   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par restructuring the Chinese economy so that domestic consumption demand  not\par exports  fuels China's growth. Broad structural changes are necessary to have\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 a major impact on our trade deficit with China. The next round of the SED will take place here in Washington in May. I understand that your constituents are very concerned about the impact of our relationship with China on their jobs and their livelihoods. I want to work with you as I prepare for these discussions. China is a big and important part of the world economy. It needs a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  whose value is determined in an open, competitive marketplace, and an economy that supports more balanced, stable growth. I look forward to working with the members of this distinguished committee on the many important issues we have before us. And I now welcome your questions." [20070131 152001 GMT]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-TREASURY-TEXT|LANGEN|Table|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|MNI|ECONOMY-TREASURY-TEXT =2|LANGEN|Table|ABN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|MNI|chained  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070203e31v000do\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
