\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  weakens slightly vs dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2007\par 08:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 29 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened slightly against the dollar in calm trading late on Monday, extending losses from the previous session due to technical selling after a recent strengthening, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> was trading at 7.7767 to the dollar at 0745 GMT, down from 7.7758 at the close on Friday. It hit an intraday high of 7.7758 while the low was 7.7800.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had closed stronger for three consecutive sessions until it began a short-term correction on Friday, which dealers said was sparked by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yuan's gains.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan weakened in late Monday trading after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mid-point at 7.7836, down from Friday's 7.7817. It briefly hit 7.7680 on Thursday, the highest intra-day level since its July 2005 revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan is consolidating after the recent strengthening," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a domestic bank.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has gained a further 4.3 percent against the dollar on top of the 2.1 percent revaluation that accompanied its depegging from the U.S. dollar in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Market talk late last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be raising interest rates had no visible impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said the trader.  \par \par \uc2 Among other factors, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seeking new ways to cope with the flood of funds pouring into China's money market, said on Monday it would begin to issue three-year bills regularly in its open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursdays.  \par \par \uc2 But traders said they did not view the new step as a tightening of monetary policy but a step that could help the bank reduce excess liquidity in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Before Friday's retreat, the yuan had risen 0.44 percent since the start of this year, or at an annual rate of 5 to 6 percent, spurring market expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the yuan to rise at a faster pace this year compared with the 3.4 percent rise in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-LATE|LANGEN|ABN|M|Z|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070129e31t000le\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
