\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan weakens vs dlr on  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jiaxin  \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2007\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds market close, UBS yuan appreciation forecast)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The yuan closed weaker against the dollar on Friday with dealers saying the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading to curb the yuan's gains.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan had strengthened after economic data on Thursday bolstered expectations for a further appreciation of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <CNY=CFXS> closed at 7.7758 to the dollar, down from 7.7705 at the close on Thursday. It hit an intraday high of 7.7745 in early trade.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan was mostly quoted at 7.7745-60 in early trade, but the sudden emergence of dollar buying at nearly all these prices about 30 minutes into trading indicated a major player was preventing the yuan from rising too fast," said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 "It could be no other bank than the PBOC," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan then trended slightly lower, hitting an intraday low of 7.7795 around midday before trimming some losses late in the session.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said most banks were unwilling to stand on the opposite side of the market from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it moved to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Before Friday's retreat, the yuan had risen 0.44 percent since the start of this year, or at an annual rate of 5 to 6 percent, spurring market expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the yuan to rise at a faster pace this year compared with the 3.4 percent rise in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems to be a temporary move from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's side as expectations had strengthed too much after yesterday's macroeconomic data," said a foreign bank dealer in Shanghai.  \par \par \uc2 INFLATION DATA  \par \par \uc2 The yuan hit an intraday high of 7.7680 in late trade on Thursday, its highest level since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was revalued by 2.1 percent and depegged from the dollar in July 2005.  \par \par \uc2 China's December consumer price inflation was 2.8 percent on the year, far exceeding economists' forecasts of about 2.0 percent and sharply higher than 1.9 percent in November.  \par \par \uc2 Although higher inflation means a nominal higher value of the yuan, dealers said expectations of a subsequent interest rate hike would narrow the gap between U.S. and Chinese rates, now at around 2.6 percent, attracting speculative funds into China.  \par \par \uc2 China is also under pressure to curb its hefty trade surplus, which rose 74 percent last year to a record $177.47 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Citing the surplus as the most important factor, UBS in a research report sent to Reuters on Friday raised the yuan's appreciation rate to 6 percent in 2007, compared with its previous forecast of 4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed swift to react to market speculation. On Friday morning, it set the yuan's mid-point at 7.7817, much lower than Thursday's post-revaluation high mid-point of 7.7735.  \par \par \uc2 Still, dealers said they were not in a rush to downgrade or upgrade their forecasts for the yuan's gains, with most maintaining their forecast that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would rise to around 7.75 to the dollar in about a month's time and appreciate 5 percent in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 For the short term, the yuan should move between 7.7620 and 7.7850, depending on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's position, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The offshore non-deliverable forward market was also little affected by the the yuan's retreat on the spot market.  \par \par \uc2 One-year NDFs <CNYNDFOR=> quoted the yuan at 7.3970/7.4000 at 0936 GMT, implying appreciation of 5.16 to 5.20 percent from Friday's mid-point. That compares with 4.96 to 5.03 percent implied by quotes on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE|LANGEN|ABN|M|Z|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070126e31q000l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw174\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2610\pichgoal600
