\par\par \b \uc2 China  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Coalition Applauds Chairman Bernanke's Recognition That China's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Undervaluation is a Distorting Subsidy  \b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2006\par 23:29\par \uc2 PR Newswire (U.S.)\par \uc2 PRN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9  2006  PR Newswire Association LLC.  All Rights Reserved.    \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Dec. 15 /PRNewswire/ -- The China \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition, which has long held that China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a distortion of free market principles, applauded Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's candor in noting that China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides an "effective subsidy" for firms that "focus on exporting." Bernanke's remarks appeared today in the prepared text of a speech delivered at the conclusion of high-level meetings in China.  \par \par \uc2 Said coalition counsel David A. Hartquist, "We are pleased that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervaluation finally has been identified by a high-ranking U.S. official for what it is: a practice that disregards global trading rules, which are essential to free and fair trade. The Federal Reserve is an independent agency, and we are pleased its analysis is supportive of our position expressed in our Section 301 petition filed in September 2004. The Bush administration rejected that petition four hours after we filed it."  \par \par \uc2 Hartquist further explained that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides a monetary windfall to its exporters, while American manufacturers must compete with products that are unfairly priced in the United States by virtue of the yuan being undervalued by as much as 40%.  \par \par \uc2 World Trade Organization (WTO) rules strictly prohibit export subsidies. Under WTO rules, importing nations can request the imposition of countervailing duties to neutralize the injurious effects of the subsidized, imported product.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. trade deficit with China, which exceeded $202 billion last year and is on track to be even larger in 2006, is a politically potent issue. Many U.S. lawmakers blame China's misalignment of its managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the imbalance, contending that an artificially weak yuan gives Chinese exporters a huge unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 On April 6th, 2005, with the strong support of the members of the coalition, Congressmen Tim Ryan (D-OH) and Duncan Hunter (R-CA) introduced H.R. 1498, a bill to clarify in U.S. trade law that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervaluation is an illegal export subsidy. On September 28, 2006, thanks to the leadership of Senator Jim Bunning (R-KY), S. 3992, the United States Fair \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Practices Act of 2006, was introduced. Both bills recognized that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervaluation is a prohibited export subsidy. China's policy on capital controls requires all exporters to exchange their U.S. dollars from trade to a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a rate dictated by the Chinese government, currently at a rate of 7.82 yuan per dollar. All three of the WTO requirements for a trade practice to be determined an illegal subsidy are met in this case; namely, (1) China's policy involves a financial contribution from the government, (2) a benefit to exporters that is (3) specific to trade.  \par \par \uc2 The China \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition is an alliance of industry, agriculture, and worker organizations whose mission is to support U.S. manufacturing by seeking an end to Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervaluation. AFL-CIO Secretary-Treasurer Richard L. Trumka and Doug Bartlett, Chairman of Bartlett Manufacturing Company, Inc., in Cary, Illinois, serve as co-chairs of the coalition. David A. Hartquist, counsel to the coalition, is a Partner at Kelley Drye Collier Shannon in Washington, D.C. and Chairman of the firm's International Trade and Customs Practice Group. For more information on the coalition, visit  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinacurrencycoalition.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.chinacurrencycoalition.org }}}\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 SOURCE China \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition  \par \par \uc2 Meg Mullery, +1-202-342-8439, or mmullery@kelleydrye.com, for China \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition  \par \par \uc2 200612151729PR_NEWS_USPR_____DCF045.xml  \par \par \uc2 Dokument PRN0000020061215e2cf00bnh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
