\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China raises required reserves for 3rd time in 2006  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eadie Chen  \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2006\par 11:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds reaction)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Nov 3 (Reuters) - China raised commercial banks' reserve requirements on Friday for the third time in five months to soak up more of the money gushing into the banking system from the country's huge balance-of-payments surplus.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) said on its Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn }}}\uc2 ) that it was increasing the proportion of deposits that banks must hold in reserve at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 0.5 percentage point, effective from Nov. 15.  \par \par \uc2 "Although the problem of excessive liquidity has been alleviated somewhat, China still faces a striking surplus in its international payments. Fresh excessive liquidity is still being generated," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in statement.  \par \par \uc2 The increase takes the standard reserve requirement to 9.0 percent for big state lenders and joint-stock banks and to 9.5 percent for smaller institutions.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced similar increases on June 16 and July 21. Each half-point rise forces banks to tie up some 150 billion yuan ($19 billion) that they could otherwise lend out.  \par \par \uc2 The PBOC has also increased interest rates twice since late April to tackle rapid credit growth and slow an economy that is on track to log double-digit growth in 2006 for the fourth year in a row.  \par \par \uc2 "For me it's a signal that the bank is really trying to do something about the ample liquidity in the system. It's a positive signal that they are trying to do something to tackle the overheating risk," said Oliver Stoenner, asset allocation strategist at Cominvest in Frankfurt.  \par \par \uc2 Market reaction was muted, not least because most China-watchers had expected the increase.  \par \par \uc2 "I think they've pretty much signalled that they've not finished tightening policy. I think it has been well telegraphed," said Jim O'Neill, chief global economist for Goldman Sachs in London.  \par \par \uc2 MONEY GUSHER  \par \par \uc2 China has a balance-of-payments surplus of about 10 percent of gross domestic product, generated principally by a fast-growing trade surplus. This is on track to exceed $140 billion in 2006, up from $102 billion in 2005, when it tripled.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in order to hold down the yuan, buys most of the dollars that flow into \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the surplus.  \par \par \uc2 This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had boosted \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $987.9 billion by the end of September.  \par \par \uc2 The PBOC issues huge volumes of bills to sterilise, or absorb, the yuan it issues in exchange for the dollars it buys.  \par \par \uc2 Economists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has done a fairly good job with these sterilisation \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but most had forecast that another increase in reserve requirements would be necessary to prevent the torrent of cash from pumping up the money supply.  \par \par \uc2 Because big banks already hold more reserves than they need to, Jun Ma with Deutsche Bank in Hong Kong said the impact of the latest increase would be insignificant.  \par \par \uc2 "It will not cause a slowdown in loan growth and shall only prevent acceleration in loan growth," Ma said.  \par \par \uc2 WHAT ABOUT THE YUAN?  \par \par \uc2 Gerard Lyons, global head of treasury research with Standard Chartered in London, said the increase dovetailed with his expectations that the PBOC is intent on jacking up required reserves towards 10 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "It doesn't suggest any imminent change in either interest rate or exchange rate policy," Lyons said.  \par \par \uc2 And that, economists say, is the problem: higher reserve requirements tackle the symptom, excess cash, but not the cause -- an ever-expanding external surplus.  \par \par \uc2 To cut off the liquidity gusher at source, they say, Beijing would need to reduce the trade surplus by, among other measures, letting the yuan rise much more sharply.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has risen just over 3 percent since it was revalued by 2.1 percent against the dollar in July 2005 and untethered from a dollar peg to float within managed bands.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. critics say the yuan remains grossly undervalued.  \par \par \uc2 "The core dilemma is that they can't really get effectively tighter conditions until the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a lot stronger," O'Neill said.  \par \par \uc2 "While the economic policy makers realise that, the politicians don't want to see an abrupt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise disrupt the export sector. The core problem is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," O'Neill added.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-REQUIREMENTS (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061103e2b3000su\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
