\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Up Midday; USD/CNY Breaks Key 7.90 Level  \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2006\par 07:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan broke above a key psychological resistance level Thursday, after the central parity rate was set lower.  \par \par \uc2 The pace of yuan appreciation has picked up from Sept. 19, the day U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson arrived in China for his first official government visit. Most dealers have been surprised by the extent of the rise and say it is likely being led by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY7.8970 around 0400 GMT, down from Wednesday's close of CNY7.9020. The U.S. dollar traded between CNY7.8995 and CNY7.8970, it's lowest level since the Chinese unit was revalued last July.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers had expected the dollar to find support at CNY7.9000, but that level was easily broken after the dollar-yuan central parity rate was set at 7.8998.  \par \par \uc2 Onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers have said the yuan's gains since Sept. 19 were likely timed for Paulson's visit.  \par \par \uc2 As at 0400 GMT Thursday, the dollar had fallen 2.6% against the yuan since the Chinese unit's revaluation. Prior to Sept. 19, its biggest fall was 2.1%.  \par \par \uc2 Since Sept. 19, the dollar-yuan central parity rate has repeatedly been set below the previous lowest trading level. Dealers suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the market isn't taking it's lead from international markets, and there has been no policy statement recently to prompt the fall.  \par \par \uc2 The daily dollar-yuan central parity rate is the weighted average of prices given by market makers, but the process is sufficiently opaque that it is unclear whether the PBOC directly manipulates the rate, or if it indirectly exerts influence over state-owned market makers by guiding the prices they give.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they are uncertain as to why the PBOC is still pushing the yuan to rise faster since Paulson's vist has ended, and whether it is likely to continue beyond this week.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan has only been in this phase for a week. Whether this is temporary or signals a new period of faster appreciation, there is no way of telling. We'll have to watch for after the holiday" starting Saturday, said a Guangzhou-based dealer.  \par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed from Saturday to Oct. 8 for China's National Day holiday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Denis McMahon, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan [ 28-09-06 0535GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060928e29s000c8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
