\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(9/22) Paulson Prods Beijing To Let Yuan Rise More  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,028 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2006\par 05:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WSJA(9/22) Paulson Prods Beijing To Let Yuan Rise More  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) By Michael M. Phillips and Andrew Browne  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise to its highest level against the dollar in more than a decade, but visiting U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson warned that the Chinese still haven't gone far enough.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday's moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were a reminder that despite Mr. Paulson's efforts this week to shift the public spotlight to the long-term U.S.-China relationship, immediate disputes over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies and trade barriers could cloud his financial diplomacy.  \par \par \uc2 "I just know that I'd like even more flexibility" in the yuan's value, Mr. Paulson said in an interview, between economic meetings with top Chinese officials.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Paulson winds up a four-day visit to China Friday by meeting with President Hu Jintao and Premier Wen Jiabao. The Treasury secretary, who built up an enormous Rolodex of Chinese contacts as chief executive of Goldman Sachs Group Inc., announced a new U.S.-China cabinet-level dialogue that, from the U.S. point of view, is a way to encourage China to pick up the pace of policy changes, including those dealing with irritants in the day-to-day Sino-American relationship.  \par \par \uc2 "My view is that the economy has now changed to the point where it's big and complex enough that I think the risk is not moving fast enough," Mr. Paulson said. He said he has tried to impress upon wary Chinese officials this week that "it's in your best interest and the rest of the world's -- and clearly in the U.S.'s best interests -- to have you move faster on the reforms."  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Paulson's counterpart in the new dialogue, Chinese Vice Premier Wu Yi, welcomed him as an "old friend of China," in a meeting before the announcement. "Among the senior officials of all governments there must only be a handful who understand China as you do," Ms. Wu said.  \par \par \uc2 Among changes Mr. Paulson advocates is liberalization of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system. The yuan is allowed to rise or fall by as much as 0.3% against the dollar from an opening rate during daily trading on an electronic market in Shanghai. However, it has rarely come close to these limits.  \par \par \uc2 In intraday trading in Shanghai Thursday, the People's Bank of China allowed the yuan to float higher against a dollar that is weakening in global markets. At one point it touched 7.9230 and closed at 7.9233. Late Wednesday in New York, the yuan was at 7.9265. The yuan has now risen by just over 2% since the 2.1% revaluation in July 2005. Over the past several weeks, the speed of appreciation has picked up.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. and other Group of Seven leading industrialized nations have been prodding China to allow a faster pace of appreciation, although their language has been guarded, and officials mostly speak of the need for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "flexibility."  \par \par \uc2 The administration of U.S. President George W. Bush has been under intense pressure from lawmakers who accuse China of keeping the yuan's value artificially low to make Chinese exports to the U.S. less expensive in dollar terms and U.S. imports more expensive in China. The U.S. Senate may vote this month on a bill -- which the administration opposes -- that would impose tariffs on Chinese goods in retaliation for Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan, speaking on the sidelines of a conference here, reiterated China's policy to eventually allow the yuan to move freely, but declined to offer a timetable.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders expect an imminent widening of the band's limits, although such a change would be largely symbolic. There already is a mechanism through which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could engineer a much faster pace of appreciation if it wanted. In after-hours trading between banks there are no limits to how far the yuan can rise or fall, and it is the banks that decide the level at which the yuan opens each trading day.  \par \par \uc2 In practice, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- with oversight from the Chinese political leadership -- decides the rate, since it is by far the largest player in the market, where it trades through state-owned commercial banks.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange markets have led to an enormous build-up in its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, with its stock of dollar assets now approaching $1 trillion. Some economists have argued that China's reserves give it a dangerous amount of leverage over the U.S. economy; should it decide to dump its dollars or even slow its purchases, the dollar could plunge and interest rates jump, according to that theory.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Paulson, however, played down those worries. "I don't see any one organization holding such a big percentage of our securities that I'm concerned," he said. "And I really don't believe that foreign investors invest in U.S. securities because they're trying to do us a favor. They invest in U.S. securities because it's in their best interests to do so, and because of the confidence they have in the U.S. economy."  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Paulson said that among other steps, he thinks China would benefit if it allowed more foreign banks, investment banks and other financial institutions to operate more freely. While he refused to comment on specific business deals now under consideration -- such as Citigroup Inc.'s long effort to buy a sizable stake in Guangdong Development Bank -- he said liberalization would help facilitate economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 "I am a huge advocate of opening up the financial markets to U.S. investment, to foreign investment -- I believe that the country is on a path to do that," Mr. Paulson said. But, he added, "there are fits and starts, and there clearly are people internally in China who are on both sides of this, as there are in every country."  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060922e29m001up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
