\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST-Caijing  \b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2006\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 Following is a summary of stories from the Aug. 21 issue of China's Caijing magazine, prepared by the magazine. Reuters has not checked the stories and does not guarantee their accuracy.  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI SCANDAL EXPOSES SOCIAL SECURITY RISKS  \par \par \uc2 Zhu Junyi, the head of the Shanghai Labor and Social Security Bureau, was fired and detained for suspected graft and mismanagement in the city's biggest government scandal in years. Several local officials and businessmen were also interrogated in relation to the case. Caijing's investigation reveals a long history of irregular investment activities by officials that have put the city's multi-billion yuan pension fund at serious risk. Since the early 1990s, Shanghai officials have repeatedly defied national regulations by investing billions from the fund in poorly managed, even illegal, projects. The bureau has acted as both regulator and manager of the fund as well, despite public demand to entrust it to market-oriented operators. So far, the fund has invested in several now-failed real estate projects, suffering millions in losses and bad debts.  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 NEW INCOME TAX LAW DELAYED  \par \par \uc2 Opposition from the Ministry of Commerce as well as eastern provinces has derailed plans to enact a law equalizing income tax rates for both domestic and foreign companies. Companies with foreign investors have enjoyed an income tax rate of 15 percent since 1991, while domestic businesses are required to pay nearly 25 percent of their income. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has worked for more than a decade to introduce a uniform tax rate, had hoped to pass the law by the end of this year. Its proposal would have unified tax rates in a bracket of 24 to 30 percent for both domestic and foreign companies. However, the National People's Congress has not included the bill in this year's legislative agenda, in part because the MOFCOM and officials in coastal regions worry that higher tax rates will alienate foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 BOC IN SOUND HEALTH: LI LIHUI  \par \par \uc2 Li Lihui, the president of the Bank of China (BOC), defended the weak performance of the bank's domestic A-shares after its spectacular IPO in July. In an interview with Caijing, Li says that BOC's financial health is improving and that he is confident the bank will reach its profit target of 33 billion yuan (US$ 4.1 billion) this year. According to Li, BOC loan growth has been slower than the industry average. In addition, the bank plans to convert between US$ 15 billion and 20 billion of its unhedged US$ 39 billion assets into RMB this year to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks. Li also said that BOC is considering offering stock options to senior executives in a new management-incentive program, which will include not only president and vice-president level officials at the bank's headquarters, but other senior officials as well.  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 ZHUHAI GOVT, CREDITORS REACH SETTLEMENT  \par \par \uc2 The Zhuhai, Guangdong, government has agreed to pay 3.11 billion yuan (US$ 389 million) to overseas creditors in August, bringing an end to a bitter five-year legal dispute. In 2001, the Zhu Kuan Group, a fundraising branch of the Zhuhai government, defaulted on its total outstanding debts of HK$ 7.33 billion, owed mostly to overseas banks. After attempts at negotiating a settlement failed, the Zhuhai government refused to cooperate with Hong Kong and Macau court orders for the liquidation of Zhu Kuan. In 2005, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Zhu Kuan's largest creditor, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the negotiations to bring both parties back to the table. A compromise was reached in early August this year. Another firm backed by the Zhuhai government will redeem the total outstanding debts, now worth more than 8 billion yuan (US$ 1 billion), with the one-off cash payment.  \par \par \uc2 - - - -  \par \par \uc2 PRESS DIGEST-Caijing|LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060822e28m0009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
