\par\par \b \uc2 China PBOC Official Tang: See 06 GDP Up More Than 10%  \b0\par\par\uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2006\par 03:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)-- Any decision on raising interest rates in China will depend on the macro-economic situation, which already shows the economy grew at a faster pace in the first half than in the first quarter, an official with China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The expansion in China's first-half gross domestic product from the year-earlier period is expected to be faster than the first quarter's 10.3% growth, said Tang Xu, director of the research bureau of the People's Bank of China.  \par \par \uc2 Tang, who was speaking on the sidelines of a financial conference, also said there is no need for the market to be nervous about the coming one-year anniversary of the yuan's revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 This month marks the first anniversary of China's new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime launched last year on July 21 when Beijing revalued the yuan 2.1% against the dollar, and scrapped its effective dollar peg to begin referencing its value to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants have been concerned China could issue fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy around this time.  \par \par \uc2 "It seldom happens that some actions are taken at anniversaries. There is no need for the market to become jittery as the anniversary of the revaluation nears," Tang said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   Corrected July 13, 2006 23:43 ET (03:43 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 14-07-06 0223GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any decision on raising interest rates in China will depend on the macro-economic situation, which already shows the economy grew at a faster pace in the first half than in the first quarter, an official with China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The expansion in China's first-half gross domestic product from the year-earlier period is expected to be faster than the first quarter's 10.3% growth, said Tang Xu, director of the research bureau of the People's Bank of China.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ("China PBOC Tang: Decision On Rate Hike Depends On Econ" published at 0223 GMT misstated the period being compared with the first quarter.) [ 14-07-06 0336GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tang said China would continue to gradually reform its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime, but that any decision on whether or not to widen the current 0.3% daily trading band on the dollar-yuan pair would depend on the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Whether China should change the current yuan trading band depends on market demand for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said. "If demand is high, the yuan is more likely to remain stable, but if the demand is low then the yuan will face greater appreciation pressure."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the past year, the yuan has appreciated only about 1.5% against the dollar and has yet to trade out to its daily limit in any single session, which traders said is because the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  steady.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tang estimated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's full-year GDP growth at more than 10%, which would be an acceleration from 2005's 9.9% gain from the year-earlier period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rise in China's consumer price index, a key inflation gauge, in the first half was likely below 2%, Tang said. For the first five months of this year, China's CPI was up 1.2%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The National Bureau of Statistics is scheduled to detail second-quarter economic data Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tang said the tightening measures the government has carried out so far this year haven't been "too strong." He said there was pressure from the market to raise interest rates, but that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will consider many factors before making any decision.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Whether to raise interest rates or not will depend on the need of the macro economy. We still cannot say China has entered a cycle of rate hikes," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said "theoretically" the room for a deposit-rate hike is relatively large because of the increased spread between rates in China and the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But when asked if China needs to simultaneously raise lending and deposit rates, Tang said the government should assess the impact of such a move on financial markets, companies and capital flows.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late April, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised benchmark lending rates 27 basis points, but left deposit rates unchanged in the first rate-hike move since October 2004. Since April the PBOC has stepped up its open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and issued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bills to designated commercial banks. It also said in June it would require banks raise their reserve requirement ratio 50 basis points effective July 5, in a concerted effort to mop up excess liquidity.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tang said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's sterilization effects have been good, but that liquidity in the banking system is still abundant. Simply relying on monetary policy to solve the problem won't be enough, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also predicted China's trade surplus this year would exceed $100 billion and reiterated China isn't aiming for more growth in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's ballooning trade surplus, which more than tripled to $102 billion last year, has underpinned the growth of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, the world's largest.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Victoria Ruan and J.R. Wu contributed to this story, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; victoria.ruan@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Theresa Davidovitz [ 14-07-06 0343GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060714e27e00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
