\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China c.bank conducts biggest drain in 12 weeks  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin  \par \uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2006\par 06:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (recasts with analysis, details)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, June 29 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mopped up a net 75 billion yuan ($9.4 billion) from the banking system this week, the biggest drain in 12 weeks, as it stepped up its tightening of money market liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued 25 billion yuan in three-month bills in its regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, after taking 110 billion yuan from the market through bill issuance and reverse repurchase agreements on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Only 60 billion yuan in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills and repos matured this week, so this week's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  resulted in a net drain of 75 billion yuan, the largest since the People's Bank of China soaked up a net 80 billion yuan in the week from April 3. Last week, it drained a net 35 billion yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a strong sign that the POBC will continue to tighten credit," said a dealer at a major state-owned bank in Beijing, adding that authorities could soon take other, stronger tightening steps such as an interest rate rise.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market still had ample liquidity, partly because of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restrain yuan appreciation. As the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it releases yuan into the market.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's largest, rose by $30 billion in May to $925 billion, the semi-official China Business News said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 This indicated aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, dealers said. Last year and in the first three months of this year, the latest months for which official data are available, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves increased on average by less than $20 billion per month.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more active on the open market this week also because funds from Bank of China's 20 billion yuan domestic share offer returned to the market in the second half of the week as shares were allocated. Last week's subscriptions to the offer froze a record 669 billion yuan.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's three-month bills jumped to 2.3399 percent on Thursday, up from 2.2588 percent in a similar \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  a week earlier and the highest level since February 17, 2005, when the yield came in at 2.3805 percent.  \par \par \uc2 It was the sixth straight week that the yield on the three-month bills rose. Dealers said they expected more rises.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-OPENMARKET-RESULT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|D|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060629e26t00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
