\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:BIS:CPI Forecast Revision May Trigger Rate Action  \b0\par\par\uc2 912 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2006\par 15:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This items updates stories published earlier Monday with further details from the BIS annual meeting, announcement of three new members of board of directors.)  \par \par \uc2 By Nikki Houston-Waesch and Martin Gelnar  \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 BASEL (Dow Jones)--Inflation forecasts in some countries may be too low and if this is the case it could affect the pace of future monetary tightening, Malcolm Knight, General Manager of the Bank for International Settlements, or BIS, said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking at a media conference in Basel at the annual meeting of BIS, known as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of central banks, Knight said inflation expectations in some countries appear to be "less well anchored in the past few months."  \par \par \uc2 "All things being equal, if going forward there is evidence that inflation expectations have risen, the policy actions of central banks will be larger than they would have been if (the expectations) remained as well anchored as they have been over the past five years," Knight said.  \par \par \uc2 Commenting on the situation in the euro zone, Knight said economic performance should improve somewhat this year. William White, a BIS economist, added: "Rates over time will have to go up to normal levels, but the pace will have to be moderate."  \par \par \uc2 White reiterated that central banks should be using a longer horizon to look at inflation. White said the duration of the processes of economic booms and busts is difficult to identify. "The upswing always tends to go longer than anybody initially thought was sustainable."  \par \par \uc2 Turning to recent turbulence in many equity and fixed-income markets, Knight said the correction appears to be due to a surge in risk aversion, adding this turbulence may prove to be beneficial.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets that have retrenched the most in the period since June 8 or so are those that saw the most spectacular increases since January to June. That isn't an unhealthy phenomenon," Knight said.  \par \par \uc2 The world economic outlook for growth and inflation is favorable, but global financial imbalances can't be sustained forever, the BIS said.  \par \par \uc2 Global imbalances are likely to decelerate in an orderly fashion, and the likelihood of risk scenarios unfolding remains low, however "the triggers for most of the financial crises observed over the last few decades were almost wholly unexpected," the BIS said.  \par \par \uc2 Following a long period of easy monetary policy, central banks in the U.S., Europe, Japan, and elsewhere have begun to tighten policy, which allows for two kinds of error, the BIS said in its latest annual report.  \par \par \uc2 "First, it could be that monetary policy should have been tightened more, and earlier," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 A long period of accommodative policy may have already embedded inflationary pressures and allowed an array of financial imbalances to mount that are becoming more threatening with time.  \par \par \uc2 "The second possible error is closely related: these imbalances might already have grown so large that monetary tightening could cause them to unwind, with negative effects on global growth and employment," the report added.  \par \par \uc2 However, countries are facing different degrees of inflationary pressures and threats of imbalances to growth.  \par \par \uc2 "For example, in continental Europe levels of excess capacity are still higher, and financial imbalances less in evidence than in the United States and a number of inflation-targeting countries, where a more advanced degree of tightening consequently appears appropriate," the report noted.  \par \par \uc2 There is a risk that imbalances unwind with a "bang" of market turbulence, a "whimper" of slow growth over an extended period, or a combination of the two, the BIS added.  \par \par \uc2 "Turning to major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, there are also grounds for concern that exchange rate movements could be large and potentially disorderly," the report said, adding that "even modest declines in the dollar could lead to heavy capital losses for large reserve holders, and this might become a matter of some political sensitivity."  \par \par \uc2 Better fiscal and structural reforms, global policy coordination and a focus on longer-term inflation trends instead of short-term quantitative targets would help to reduce risk. So would an agreement on how central banks would react in the event of crisis, ensuring national legislation doesn't impede time-critical information sharing, and less of a focus on domestic priorities.  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  "must appreciate further," and there are signs that some Asian central banks have scaled back \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, remains an important exception in that it accumulated a further $200 billion plus of reserves in the course of 2005," the report noted.  \par \par \uc2 The BIS said many of the past crises such as the Mexican crisis of 1994, the Asian crisis in 1997, and crises in Russian and the U.S. in 1998 weren't preceded by high inflation. While low inflation is desirable, it isn't always enough to ensure good economic performance.  \par \par \uc2 Financial imbalances should play a bigger role in monetary policy, it said. "Over longer time periods, such imbalances can pose an even greater and more dangerous threat to price stability," than shorter-term, more conventional inflationary pressures, the BIS added.  \par \par \uc2 In a separate statement, the BIS said it has elected three new members of the board of directors. They are Guillermo Ortiz, Governor of the Bank of Mexico, Jean-Claude Trichet, President of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Zhou Xiaochuan, governor of the People's Bank of China.  \par \par \uc2 -Nikki Houston-Waesch and Martin Gelnar, Dow Jones Newswires; +41 443 8042; martin.gelnar@dowjones.com [ 26-06-06 1302GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060626e26q000mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
