\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Yuan in focus in Chinese battle to curb lending   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao and Tamora Vidaillet   \par \uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2006\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, June 19 (Reuters) - China's latest move to rein in liquidity and balance growth will likely have a limited impact, with some arguing Beijing must let the yuan appreciate more rapidly to tackle the root cause of the economy's problems.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the amount of cash that banks have to hold in reserve by 0.5 percentage points on Friday, after economic data for May showed frenzied lending continuing to fuel excessive investment and a runaway economy.   \par \par \uc2 The move, along with higher interest rates and measures to curb property speculation, could help keep the economy on an even keel in the short term.   \par \par \uc2 But unless the yuan, which has appreciated just 1.34 percent since its 2.1 percent revaluation last July, is allowed to rise more aggressively, analysts worry that China risks pursuing myopic solutions that do not address the source of the excess cash in its financial system.   \par \par \uc2 "Raising bank reserve requirements may help tighten liquidity in the near term, but it cannot fundamentally prevent rampant liquidity," said Jiming Ha, an economist with China International Capital Corp.   \par \par \uc2 "The linchpin to resolve current economic problem is adjusting the yuan exchange rate," he wrote in a research note.   \par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , under pressure from growing money supply as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan's big rises, wants the yuan to rise faster, other parts of the government fear an adverse impact on the rural poor and jobs.   \par \par \uc2 Along with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade surplus and massive foreign investment inflows also put pressure on money supply.   \par \par \uc2 In a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keen to allow the yuan to gain strength, it set its daily benchmark reference rate below 8.0 to the dollar for two consecutive days last week and allowed it to scale an intraday high of 7.9970 on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 GRADUAL PACE   \par \par \uc2 Beijing has taken a slew of measures since mid-2003, including using some heavy-handed administrative steps in 2004, but the latest campaign is more relying on economic levers, which also raises hopes that it may let the yuan rise, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Rob Subbaraman of Lehman Brothers said China should raise interest rates more sharply and, to do so, Beijing needs to make the yuan more flexible and allow it to strengthen.   \par \par \uc2 "We think that probably China will allow the yuan to appreciate but we don't expect it to be a major policy instrument," said Subbaraman, who expects the yuan to appreciate to 7.7 against the dollar by the end of this year.   \par \par \uc2 "We think it's going to be a very gradual pace of appreciation and not significant enough to have much of an effect on the economy, at least for this year."   \par \par \uc2 U.S. research firm High Frequency Economics estimated the rise in banks' required reserve ratio would increase reserves by 6.7 percent and potentially trim lending growth by around 2.6 percentage points to a more palatable 16.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2 If Beijing does manage to reverse the uptrend in credit growth, analysts say it may be able to rely on issuing bills to mop up liquidty and directives to lenders to keep further lending growth in check.   \par \par \uc2 The next move will depend on the state of an economy which grew by 10.3 percent on the year in the first quarter.   \par \par \uc2 "I think what we'll see is probably about 6 weeks or so of sitting tight to see whether the current measures are having a real impact," said Arthur Kroeber, managing director of Dragonomics, a Hong Kong-based economic research firm.   \par \par \uc2 "If they're not, or not enough, then you'd see another hike (in reserve requirements) but I don't necessarily think that they will be forced into it," he said.   \par \par \uc2 A further rise in rates was also possible, although some are scepticial about the effectiveness of such a move in an immature economy.   \par \par \uc2 Frank Gong, an economist at JP Morgan Chase Bank in Hong Kong, expects a combination of tightening measures in the months ahead, including further yuan strength, and agrees China should fine-tune its approach.   \par \par \uc2 "Without addressing the fundamental problem of excess liquidity, which ultimately arises from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, it will be hard for the authorities to find effective market-based tools to slow the economy," he said.   \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-POLICY (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060622e26j000n6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
